{"type":"message","id":"6cd969de","parentId":"a1f314dd","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:36:36.413Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_96917a25c2474058872dc003","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 32\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 raman raman 4096 Dec 21  2025 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 8 raman raman 4096 Dec 21  2025 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 raman raman 4096 Dec 21  2025 gello\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 raman raman    0 Dec 21  2025 __init__.py\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 raman raman 5457 Dec 21  2025 lerobot_agent.py\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 raman raman 3544 Dec 21  2025 openpi_agent.py\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 raman raman 2164 Dec 21  2025 openvla_agent.py\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 raman raman 3866 Dec 21  2025 spacemouse_agent.py\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1782401796413}}
{"type":"message","id":"d6a2d19c","parentId":"6cd969de","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:36:36.413Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_e7c3f7093ff649d788e4bb79","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# robot-imitation-glue\n\nFramework to reduce engineering efforts in using imitation learning for robot manipulation.\n\nFacilitates:\n- Dataset Collection (Lerobot dataset format) through **teleoperation** (Gello and Spacemouse supported but you can easily add others)\n- Modifying/filtering **Lerobot Datasets**\n- Replaying datasets on real hardware (for debugging)\n- **Evaluating trained policies** on real hardware and save rollout observations(supports Lerobot ACT/ DP, OpenVLA, Pi0 and Pi-Fast but you can easily add others)\n\nEverything is visualized using Rerun.\n\n\nThe code is written modularly so that you can easily:\n\n1) integrate a new hardware setup\n2) evaluate new learning algorithms\n3) integrate new teleoperation methods\n\n\n## Some core design decisions and assumptions\n\n- code to describe and train networks for policy is not part of this codebase, recommended flow is to collect dataset here, train in separate codebase and then evalute the trained policy using a webserver (to avoid dependency issues and maintain separation of concerns).\n\n- policies and teleop devices are both abstracted as agents.\n\n- we assume a single robot arm + gripper. Control interface is absolute end-effector poses + absolute gripper width.\n\n- we use the Lerobot dataset format and tooling to store demonstrations and rollouts.\n\n- Callbacks are provided to switch between different action formats for the dataset/ policies:\n![](docs/action-flow.png)\n\n\n## Examples\n\n2 examples are available of how to integrate this codebase for your robot.\nBoth are powered using our airo-mono repo, but you can freely select a control stack for your hardware.\n\n### UR5station\n\n### UR3station\n\n\n## Development\n\n### installation\n\n- clone this repo\n- initialize and update submodules: `git submodule update --init`\n- create the conda environment `conda env create -f environment.yaml`\n\n\n## Dataset visualisation:\n\npython lerobot/lerobot/scripts/visualize_dataset_html.py   --root datasets/2025-04-29_15-40-01/ --force-override 1 --repo-id test/test\n\nIn the above, the --repo-id doesn't matter, so literally test/test is fine.\n\n## Preprocessing\n\nrobot_imitation_glue > ur5station > prepare_datasets.py\n\nRedo visualisation above\n\n## Dataset merging\n\npython lerobot/lerobot/scripts/merge.py --sources datasets_tmp/clothes-hanger-v3p5-EVAL-successes-visionOnly/ datasets_tmp/clothes-hanger-v3p5-rollouts4augmentation-visionOnly/ datasets_tmp/clothes-hanger-v3p6-w500h720-2cam-visionOnly/ --output datasets_tmp/clothes-hanger-v8-visionAugmentedByInstrRollouts --max_dim 7 --fps 10\n\n## Training\n\npython lerobot/lerobot/scripts/train.py --config_path=robot_imitation_glue/ur5station/lerobot_train/...config.json\n\npython lerobot/lerobot/scripts/train.py --config_path=robot_imitation_glue/uR3station/lerobot_train/pick-block-diffusion-config.json\n\n(extra info in lerobot > examples > advanced > 4_train_policy_with_script)\n\nmodel size parameters in config: \"down_dims\", \"kernel_size\", \"diffusion_step_embed_dim\",\n\n\nscreen -S session_name -> starts session\nctrl+a ctrl+d -> detaches session\nscreen -Rd session_name -> reattaches session\n\n## Evaluation\n\nrobot_imitation_glue > ur5station > eval_diffusion_lerobot.py\n\nhf upload ramen-noodels/test_5_episodes ./datasets/test_5_episodes --repo-type=dataset\n\nhf download ramen-noodels/test_5_episodes --repo-type dataset --local-dir datasets/test_5_episodes"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1782401796413}}
{"type":"message","id":"21107197","parentId":"d6a2d19c","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:36:47.436Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the setup:\n- Robot: UR5 or UR3 arm + gripper (single arm)\n- Framework: robot-imitation-glue with lerobot\n- Hardware includes: camera (ipc_camera), force-torque sensor (ipc_ft), microphone (ipc_mic), button detector\n- Modalities: vision + audio (microphone) + possibly force-torque\n- Training: lerobot (ACT/Diffusion Policy)\n- Evaluation: real hardware rollouts\n\nThe user's research is about instrumentation (audio from microphone presumably) helping policy performance. They want to compare:\n\n\n1. Policy trained from scratch\n2. Policy where audio or image encoder is pretrained with instrumentation data\n3. Policy where encoder uses generic pretrained weights\n\nI'm ready to start exploring the decision tree systematically. The most foundational question is about the instrumentation modality itself—since their setup includes microphone, camera, and force-torque sensors, I need to clarify what \"instrumentation\" means in their context, particularly whether the audio channel is specifically detecting contact events like button presses.\n\nFor the control approach, they're asking whether tasks will be scripted or learned end-to-end through imitation learning—this determines whether the policy is following a predefined trajectory or learning directly from demonstrations. They also need to decide on the number of real-world rollout trials for evaluation; in robotics, 30 trials per task is common in conferences like CoRL and ICRA, though this ranges from 20 to 50 depending on the venue and setup.\n\nNow I should start asking them the first concrete question to understand their specific setup better.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nGood — I can see you're working with a **UR3/UR5 arm + gripper**, using **lerobot** for training (ACT/Diffusion Policy), with modalities including **camera, microphone (ipc_mic), and force-torque sensor (ipc_ft)**. The phd folder references \"MagTouch2\" and button pressing — so this ties into instrumented contact detection via audio.\n\nLet me start grilling, one question at a time.\n\n---\n\n## Question 1: What exactly is \"instrumentation\" in your setup?\n\nYou mention comparing an encoder pretrained on \"instrumentation data\" vs. \"generic pretrained weights.\" But the term \"instrumentation\" is overloaded. Based on your codebase I see three potential instrumental signals:\n\n1. **Audio (microphone)** — contact sounds, friction noise, etc.\n2. **Force-torque sensor** — direct contact force measurements\n3. **Something else?**\n\n**My recommendation:** Be explicit. If your instrumentation is **audio contact signals** (which seems to be the case given `ipc_mic.py` and the MagTouch2 paper), then state that clearly. The claim becomes: *\"Contact audio as an instrumentation signal improves data-efficient policy learning compared to generic pretraining.\"*\n\nWhich modality is your instrumentation, and is it a single modality or a combination?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":14613,"output":619,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15232,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782401796414,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b5bdfbe281aea38f"}}
{"type":"message","id":"9b5d62fb","parentId":"21107197","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:45:25.530Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"no, with instrumentation I mean adding sensors to the robots environment, for example for a simple task of pressing buttons the button itself is our sensor and we can read out if it is pressed or not. for the bottle opening task it is suitable for putting an ldr inside the bottle cap and the ldr gives us a measure of how open the cap is. This privileged information is available during training but not during inference. So this has to be integrated in the policy, possibly through pretraining the audio or vision encoder."}],"timestamp":1782402325529}}
{"type":"message","id":"9e151b56","parentId":"9b5d62fb","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:45:42.123Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a crucial clarification. The user's \"instrumentation\" is NOT about adding audio sensors - it's about adding task-specific sensors (like button state sensors, LDR light sensors in bottle caps) that provide privileged/auxiliary information during training only. This information is NOT available at test time.\n\nSo the paradigm is:\n1. During training: policy has access to extra sensor readings (button pressed/not pressed, LDR brightness = how open cap is)\n2. During inference: policy does NOT have access to these sensors\n\nThe claim is that having this auxiliary supervision signal during training improves the policy's performance when tested without it - essentially it's a form of self-supervised pretraining or auxiliary loss that teaches the encoder better representations.\n\n\nNow I'm working through how this privileged information actually gets integrated into the policy architecture. The key insight is that the encoder needs to learn from both the observable inputs and the privileged signals during training, so that at inference time the encoder has already learned richer representations that generalize better. The bottleneck architecture approach makes sense - the encoder learns to predict the privileged signal as an auxiliary task, forcing it to extract the underlying features that matter, then during inference it only needs the standard observations since those representations are already embedded in the encoder weights.\n\nFor the experimental design, I'm mapping out how each task would have its own privileged signal (binary button state for pressing, continuous light intensity for bottle opening) and how the three variants would differ: baseline training on observations only, then the two pretrained variants where the encoder is first trained to predict the privileged signal before being fine-tuned on the main task.\n\nThe critical question is clarifying the hypothesis - it's not that pretraining alone helps, but specifically that instrumentation-derived pretraining is what drives the data efficiency gains. This means the instrumentation variant should show significantly better performance than both the from-scratch baseline and the generic pretrained variant, especially when training data is reduced.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nAh, this changes things significantly! **Instrumentation = task-specific auxiliary sensors providing privileged information during training only.** This is a fundamentally different paradigm from what I initially assumed.\n\nLet me make sure I understand correctly before moving to the next question:\n\n- **Button pressing** → button has built-in electrical contact sensor → binary signal (pressed/not pressed)\n- **Bottle opening** → LDR inside cap → continuous signal (how open the cap is)\n- This privileged signal is used as **auxiliary supervision** during encoder pretraining\n- At **inference time**, the policy has NO access to these sensors\n- The hypothesis: learning to predict these instrumented signals forces the encoder to learn task-relevant representations that generalize better\n\n**So the mechanism is:** the encoder pre-trained on predicting instrumented signals develops better internal features → when fine-tuned for the policy task with limited data, it needs fewer demonstrations to reach the same performance.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2: How exactly is the privileged information fed into the encoder during pretraining?\n\nYou said \"pretraining the audio or vision encoder\" — but the instrumentation signals are **not audio**. They're electrical/optical sensor readings (binary button state, continuous LDR value). So the pretraining setup would be:\n\n> **Input:** Image (or image + robot state) → **Output:** Instrumentation value (button state, LDR reading)\n\nThis is essentially **supervised representation learning** where the instrumentation is the label.\n\nA few sub-questions here:\n\n**2a. Which encoder gets pretrained — image encoder only, or also the robot-state encoder?**\nMy recommendation: **Image encoder only.** The instrumentation signal is about the environment state (is the button pressed? how open is the cap?), which is primarily visible in images. This also makes the cleanest comparison with \"generic pretrained image encoder\" (e.g., ImageNet-pretrained ViT/ResNet).\n\n**2b. What's the pretraining objective?** Classification (button: binary) or regression (LDR: continuous)? Or should you normalize everything to a unified regression target?\nMy recommendation: **Normalize to a unified formulation.** Use regression with normalized targets ∈ [0,1] for all tasks. Binary button = 0 or 1. LDR = normalized brightness. This keeps the pretraining protocol consistent across tasks, which is crucial for a clean paper narrative.\n\n**2c. How much pretraining data do you need, and how does it relate to the policy training data?** Is it the same demonstrations, or separately collected?\n\nWhat's your thinking on 2c?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":15347,"output":941,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":16288,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782402325531,"responseId":"chatcmpl-95e0a444c89b231d"}}
{"type":"message","id":"6d8530f6","parentId":"9e151b56","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:57:29.010Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"for 2a, I think this is actually task dependant, because for example the 2mm that a button goes up and down makes a difference between simply toucheing the button and actually presseing it, this will also have an impact on how much force you exert on the button, which we cwould like to minimize. So here the audio encoder is much better suited because click sound of the button is very visible in the spectogram and so audio data is much better suited to learn the privileged information. However for the other takss I need your help to see which is better. For image feneric weights I can use imagenet indeed, for audio encoder generic weights I could use for example AudioSet weights. for 2b, the pretraining objective goes hand in hand with the type on instrumentation. if the output of the instrumentation is binary or discrete states, then classification, if the output of the instrumentation, so the privileged infromation is continuous then regression. but indeed normalizeing the regression task can be considered. 2c yes, so the pretraining data is the actual demonstration data. so not separately collected."}],"timestamp":1782403049009}}
{"type":"message","id":"490a2d64","parentId":"6d8530f6","timestamp":"2026-06-25T15:57:46.970Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, this is getting clearer. Let me digest:\n\n1. The encoder choice (audio vs image) is task-dependent, based on which modality better captures the instrumentation signal\n2. The pretraining objective matches the instrumentation type (classification for discrete, regression for continuous)\n3. Pretraining data = the same demonstration data used for policy training\n\nNow let me think about the task-instrumentation-encoder mapping:\n\n- **Button pressing**: instrumentation = button state sensor (binary pressed/not pressed). Audio is better because click sound is distinct in spectrogram. Encoder = audio.\n- **Bottle opening**: instrumentation = LDR in cap (continuous, how open). This is visual (light through cap). Encoder = image.\n\n\n- **Ziplock bag opening**: instrumentation could be distance sensor or light sensor through the bag opening. Encoder = image (probably).\n- **Wiping task**: instrumentation could be force sensor or pressure sensor on the wiping area. Encoder = ? This one is harder. Could be audio (consistent friction sound), or force-torque.\n\nBut there's a tension here I need to flag: the pretraining data IS the demonstration data used for policy training. So if you have 100 demonstration episodes and you use all 100 to pretrain the encoder, then train the policy on those same 100 episodes, the encoder has already seen exactly the distribution it'll encounter during policy fine-tuning. That's actually a form of supervised pretraining rather than self-supervised learning.\n\nThis raises a question about the baseline comparison. The \"from scratch\" variant trains both the encoder and policy jointly from the demonstrations, which means the encoder learns whatever features help the policy task. So why would pretraining on instrumentation labels give a meaningful advantage? The only way this makes sense is if the policy training data is significantly smaller than the pretraining data—or if the instrumentation signal provides a much clearer learning signal than the policy objective alone. But if they're using the same dataset, the difference between pretraining on instrumentation vs. learning from the policy objective directly might not be substantial.\n\nUnless the instrumentation objective is fundamentally easier to learn from, forcing the encoder to develop better representations that transfer to the policy task even when both see identical data. It's an unusual setup, but it could work if the auxiliary supervision is more informative than the policy gradients alone. I should push back on this experimental design to clarify whether the advantage comes from the pretraining objective itself or from data scale.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThis is getting interesting. But I have a concern about the experimental design that I need to surface before we continue.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3: The \"same data\" problem\n\nYou said pretraining data = demonstration data. This means **all three variants see the exact same images and audio** — the only difference is whether the encoder was first trained with the instrumentation signal as a label.\n\nLet me make the concern concrete. With 100 demonstrations:\n\n| Variant | Encoder sees 100 demos | Policy trained on 100 demos |\n|---|---|---|\n| **From scratch** | ✓ (jointly with policy) | ✓ |\n| **Instrumentation pretrained** | ✓ (pretraining phase) | ✓ (fine-tuning phase) |\n| **Generic pretrained** | N/A (frozen or fine-tuned) | ✓ |\n\nThe from-scratch variant's encoder also learns from the same 100 demonstrations. The question is: **why would an extra pretraining pass on the same data with instrumentation labels meaningfully help over joint training?**\n\nIn standard transfer learning, the pretraining dataset is orders of magnitude larger than the downstream dataset. Here they're identical. This is more like an **auxiliary loss** setup than transfer learning.\n\n**My concern:** Reviewers will ask \"why not just add the instrumentation as an auxiliary loss during policy training?\" If you pretrain then fine-tune, you're essentially doing two-stage training on the same data — which is often *worse* than joint training (the encoder may forget policy-relevant features during pretraining).\n\n**My recommendation:** You have two cleaner designs:\n\n**Option A — True auxiliary supervision during policy training:** Add instrumentation prediction as an auxiliary loss head during policy training. Compare: (1) no auxiliary loss, (2) auxiliary loss with instrumentation labels, (3) auxiliary loss with random labels. This is the simplest design and directly tests whether the instrumentation signal improves representation learning.\n\n**Option B — Keep two-stage but justify it:** If you keep pretrain-then-finetune, argue that the instrumentation task is a much easier learning problem (direct labels) that bootstraps better features before the harder policy learning task. You'd need ablation showing pretraining iterations matter.\n\nWhich direction resonates more with what you had in mind? Or did you envision something else entirely?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":16528,"output":1013,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":17541,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782403049011,"responseId":"chatcmpl-89c9ada4082c3eef"}}
{"type":"message","id":"73ff9948","parentId":"490a2d64","timestamp":"2026-06-25T16:09:26.507Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I had option B in mind. Because you can immediately see if the modality is suitable to predict the privileged information, however with option A you don't actually need to chose which encoder or modality is pretrained with the privileged information, however if the audio is not suitable I would opt for removeing the audio modality completely? or what do you suggest? and how do I determine if it is suitable or not?  but what exactly do you mean with ablations that show pretraining iterations matter? I thought compareing the from scratch policy with the instrumentation pretraining and the generic pretraining shows that it matters?"}],"timestamp":1782403766506}}
{"type":"message","id":"ed2f7e02","parentId":"73ff9948","timestamp":"2026-06-25T16:09:48.573Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking good clarifying questions. Let me break down what they're asking:\n\n1. If a modality is not suitable for predicting privileged info, should they remove it entirely or what?\n2. How to determine suitability?\n3. What do I mean by \"ablations showing pretraining iterations matter\"?\n4. They think the 3-way comparison (from scratch vs instrumentation vs generic) already shows it matters.\n\nLet me address these:\n\nFor the \"pretraining iterations matter\" ablation - what I meant was: does the encoder actually benefit from the pretraining phase, or is it just the fine-tuning that matters? For example:\n- Pretrain for 1000 steps → finetune\n- Pretrain for 10000 steps → finetune  \n- Pretrain for 100000 steps → finetune\n\n\n- No pretraining (from scratch) → finetune\n\nIf performance improves monotonically with more pretraining iterations, that proves the pretraining phase itself is contributing (not just the architecture or data exposure). If 1000 steps and 10000 steps give the same result, maybe the encoder just needs one pass through the data and the instrumentation labels don't add much.\n\nBut the user makes a fair point: their main comparison already shows whether instrumentation pretraining helps vs. no pretraining vs. generic pretraining. That's the core result. The iterations ablation would be supplementary.\n\nNow, for the modality suitability question — how do you know if audio is actually good at predicting the instrumented signal? The user mentioned that for button pressing, audio might be better than other modalities.\n\nI should test pretraining accuracy as a quick sanity check: split off a validation set, pretrain the encoder on the instrumentation signal, and measure how well it predicts. If audio achieves 95% accuracy on button state but image only reaches 60%, that's a clear signal audio is the right modality. But if audio maxes out at 55% (barely above random for binary classification), it's not capturing the signal meaningfully. The key question is what to do when a modality proves unsuitable — the user asked whether to remove audio entirely if it doesn't work for a particular task. In option B's two-stage design, this gets tricky because you'd need to handle modality selection differently.\n\nIf audio pretraining doesn't learn useful representations for a task, you'd fall back to the from-scratch variant, which trains the audio encoder jointly with the policy and learns only policy-relevant features. So you don't need to remove the modality — the from-scratch baseline already handles that case gracefully. The three-way comparison actually covers all three scenarios: from scratch learns only what the policy needs, instrumentation pretrained learns instrumented features from the best modality, and generic pretrained brings external knowledge to the table.\n\nBut the user's core question is still open: how do they determine which modality — audio or image — is better suited for a given task? That's the next thing to tackle.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nGood questions. Let me address them:\n\n**On \"pretraining iterations matter\":** You're right that your 3-way comparison is the core result. What I meant by the ablation was secondary — something like: pretrain for 1K steps vs 10K steps vs 100K steps, then fine-tune each. If more pretraining = better policy, it proves the pretraining *phase* contributes something beyond just seeing the data once. But this is supplementary. Your main 3-variant comparison is sufficient as the primary result.\n\n**On what to do if a modality isn't suitable:** You don't need to remove it. The from-scratch variant already handles that — the encoder learns only policy-relevant features. The question is about which encoder to pretrain for the *instrumentation variant*.\n\n---\n\n## Question 4: How do you decide which modality (audio vs. image) to pretrain for each task?\n\nThis is a design decision that needs a principled criterion, otherwise reviewers will say \"you cherry-picked the modality that worked.\" You need a **pre-registered suitability test** — something measured *before* the policy evaluation that objectively picks the modality.\n\nHere's my recommended approach:\n\n**Suitability metric = pretraining prediction accuracy on a held-out validation split.**\n\nFor each task:\n1. Split demonstrations: 80% train, 20% validation\n2. Pretrain **image encoder** → predict instrumentation → measure validation accuracy/R²\n3. Pretrain **audio encoder** → predict instrumentation → measure validation accuracy/R²\n4. Pick the modality with higher score\n\nThis gives you a table like:\n\n| Task | Instrumentation | Image R²/Acc | Audio R²/Acc | Chosen |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Button pressing | Binary (pressed) | ? | ? | Audio (presumably) |\n| Bottle opening | Continuous (LDR) | ? | ? | Image (presumably) |\n| Ziplock bag | ? | ? | ? | ? |\n| Wiping | ? | ? | ? | ? |\n\n**But this raises the next question:** What is the instrumentation for ziplock bag and wiping? And for bottle opening — is the LDR signal better captured in the image (you can visually see how open the cap is) or is it truly privileged info not visible in the image?\n\n**My recommendation:** The instrumentation should be **privileged information that is NOT directly readable from the image or audio**. If the image already shows \"cap is 50% open,\" then an image encoder can learn that without the LDR label — and your instrumentation pretraining adds nothing over from-scratch. The LDR must provide something the camera *can't* see (e.g., internal light transmission through threads that the camera angle misses).\n\nSo: **what is the instrumentation for each of the 4 tasks, and why is it privileged (not directly observable from standard sensors)?**"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":17676,"output":1287,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":18963,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782403766509,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b226e1e4e3d54059"}}
{"type":"message","id":"59e09233","parentId":"ed2f7e02","timestamp":"2026-06-30T09:52:12.662Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have to comment on your statement, If the image already shows \"cap is 50% open,\" then an   \n image encoder can learn that without the LDR label — and your instrumentation pretraining adds nothing over from-scratch.  Yes it can learn that without the LDR label, but the hypothesis is that it will learn to interpret it faster if supervised training with instrumentation is used. from scratch will eventually also learn it, but potentially needs more training data. The instrumented data should be visible in the audio spectogram or camera otherwise the model will just learn spurious correlations or nothing at all. For the putting rubber plugs in metal sheets I was thinking about putting a metal ring around the plug end, and somehow using it as a circuit closer on the metal sheet so it becaumes similar to reading out a button press, for the bottle opening I was thinking about putting an LDR in it. For the ziplock bag I was thinking about a linear potentiometer but I just looked them up and they look very rigid so not really suitable. for the disk wipeing task also just measuring electrical contact by adding wires to the setup, but if you have other ideas or suggestions, they are welcome"}],"timestamp":1782813132657}}
{"type":"message","id":"f5b04b5c","parentId":"59e09233","timestamp":"2026-06-30T09:52:35.285Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user makes an important clarification: the instrumentation signal IS visible in the image/audio, but the hypothesis is that supervised learning with labels makes the encoder learn faster (more data-efficient). This is actually a much cleaner hypothesis. Let me reframe:\n\n**Refined hypothesis:** Supervised pretraining on visible-but-labeled instrumentation signals teaches the encoder to focus on task-relevant visual/audio features faster than unsupervised (from-scratch) learning, resulting in better data efficiency during policy fine-tuning.\n\nThis is actually well-aligned with semi-supervised learning literature — even when labels are for features visible in the data, supervised pretraining acts as an inductive bias that speeds up learning.\n\nNow let me think about the instrumentation ideas:\n\n1. **Rubber plugs in metal sheet**: Metal ring around plug → circuit closure when plugged in → binary signal (inserted/not inserted). Good, similar to button pressing.\n\n\n2. **Bottle opening**: LDR in cap → continuous signal based on light transmission as threads rotate. The challenge is correlating this with the actual opening angle, but it's a reasonable continuous signal.\n3. **Ziplock bag**: Linear potentiometer seems too rigid for this application. I'm considering alternatives like a Hall effect sensor with a magnet to measure slider position, or an encoder wheel attached to the zip mechanism.\n4. **Disk wiping**: Electrical contact works, though it's not entirely clear what aspect of the wiping task this would measure.\n\nFor the ziplock bag specifically, I'm thinking through simpler solutions — a potentiometer arranged as a string potentiometer where pulling the slider winds string around a spindle, or using a load cell under the bag to detect position changes as the slider moves. The key constraint is that the sensor needs to move naturally with the zip mechanism without adding rigidity or interfering with the task itself.\n\nNow I'm realizing the core question isn't just about which tasks need instrumentation, but rather which modality would best capture the signal for each task. For the wiping task specifically, I need to clarify what's actually being measured — whether it's about detecting contact, ensuring consistent pressure, or verifying complete coverage of the area.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nGood clarification. So the refined hypothesis is:\n\n> **Supervised pretraining on visible-but-labeled instrumentation signals acts as an inductive bias, teaching the encoder to focus on task-relevant features faster than unsupervised learning. This yields better data efficiency during policy fine-tuning.**\n\nThis is clean and well-aligned with semi-supervised learning literature. The signal doesn't need to be hidden — it just needs a label to accelerate learning.\n\nNow, your instrumentation ideas are creative but some need refinement. Let me focus on the tasks that need help:\n\n---\n\n## Question 5: Instrumentation for ziplock bag and wiping — what's the right sensor?\n\nLet me evaluate your proposals and suggest alternatives:\n\n**Ziplock bag opening:**\n- Your idea: Linear potentiometer → too rigid, will change the dynamics of the bag\n- The question to answer: \"How far is the zip open?\" (continuous, 0-100%)\n- **My suggestions:**\n  1. **String potentiometer** — a spool with a thin string attached to the zip slider. As the slider moves, string unwinds, rotating the spool. Very low resistance, captures continuous position.\n  2. **Hall effect sensor + magnet** — tiny magnet embedded in the zip slider, stationary Hall sensor underneath. Measures distance → continuous open amount. Virtually zero mechanical interference.\n  3. **Capacitive distance sensor** — same principle, no magnet needed.\n\n**My recommendation: Hall effect sensor + magnet.** Minimal intrusion, continuous readout, cheap, easy to calibrate.\n\n**Disk wiping:**\n- Your idea: Electrical contact → but what does this measure? That the disk touched the surface? That seems too binary and doesn't capture \"consistent pressure\" or \"coverage.\"\n- The question to answer: Is it \"was the area wiped properly?\" or \"is the wiping contact consistent?\"\n- **My suggestions:**\n  1. **Load cell under the wiping surface** — measures downward force during wiping. Captures \"not too hard, not too soft.\" Continuous signal.\n  2. **Conductive coating on the wiping area** — like a resistive touch grid. The wiping disk (with conductive material) completes circuits as it passes over. Measures coverage area. Binary per-cell → aggregate to % coverage.\n  3. **Force-sensing resistor (FSR) under the disk** — direct pressure measurement at the contact point.\n\n**My recommendation for wiping: Load cell under the wiping surface.** Measures force continuously → directly maps to \"consistent pressure.\" Also: does this task need bimanual execution (one arm holds the disk, one arm stabilizes the surface)?\n\n**And the meta-question:** Which modality captures each best?\n\n| Task | Instrumentation | Best modality? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rubber plugs | Circuit closure (binary) | **Audio** — click/snap sound when plug seats |\n| Bottle opening | LDR in cap (continuous) | **Image** — LDR correlates with visible cap rotation angle |\n| Ziplock bag | Hall effect (continuous) | **?** — audio (zip sound changes as it opens)? image (visible slider position)? |\n| Disk wiping | Load cell (continuous) | **?** — audio (friction sound varies with pressure)? image (visible compression)? |\n\nFor ziplock and wiping — which modality do you think captures the signal best, and why?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":19083,"output":1178,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":20261,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782813132761,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9381781ed65d42de"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b010e05c","parentId":"f5b04b5c","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:15:50.368Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"for the ziplock bag I'm not convinced yet by the hall effect sensor or the other ideas you had, I will discuss this later with my promotor, for the disk wipeing the loadcell under the wiping area looks interesting, the issue with measureing coverage is that I'll be using diffusion policy and it doesn't have memory so I'm not sure if this coverage will be an issue what do you think? force senseing resistor under the disk is also interesteing however will we measure delta forces or absolute measurements, because different materials will need different forces? so how will these generalize? similar question for the load cell. for zip lock bag I think image, and for disk wiping I think audio, but we will need to see this from the suitability metric you mentioned earlier."}],"timestamp":1782814550366}}
{"type":"message","id":"7c0b21f9","parentId":"b010e05c","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:16:03.771Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user raises some good points:\n\n1. Ziplock bag instrumentation - undecided, will discuss with promotor. OK, we can park this.\n\n2. Diffusion policy has no memory - so coverage measurement doesn't make sense as a cumulative signal. This is a valid point. With diffusion policy (autoregressive but no hidden state), each timestep is independent. So the instrumentation signal needs to be about the current state, not cumulative history.\n\n3. Load cell/FSR - delta vs absolute forces. Good concern. If the policy is trained on one material and tested on another, absolute force values won't transfer. But normalized force (relative to max force during demo) could work.\n\n4. Modality selection - image for ziplock, audio for wiping, but will verify with suitability metric. Good.\n\n\nThe core challenge here is generalization across materials - if we train on absolute force measurements, the policy won't transfer to different surfaces or objects. But if we normalize the force signal relative to what's observed during training, the policy learns to recognize proportional force patterns instead of fixed thresholds, which should generalize better.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nGood catches on all of these. Let me address them:\n\n**On diffusion policy having no memory:** You're right. Diffusion policy is autoregressive per-timestep with no recurrent hidden state. So the instrumentation signal must be **instantaneous** — a property of the current state, not cumulative history. Coverage is cumulative (how much area has been wiped over time), so it doesn't fit. Force or contact state at the current timestep fits perfectly.\n\n**On absolute vs. relative force measurements:** This is a real generalization concern. If you train with a load cell under a glass surface and test on wood, absolute force values shift.\n\n**My recommendation:** Normalize all instrumentation signals to **[0,1] relative to the per-episode range**:\n\n- `normalized_force = (force - force_min_episode) / (force_max_episode - force_min_episode)`\n\nOr better yet, normalize to **percentile within the demonstration dataset**. This way the model learns \"is the force high or low relative to what's typical for this task?\" rather than \"is the force 5.2N?\" This generalizes across materials and surfaces.\n\nBut this leads to the real question:\n\n---\n\n## Question 6: What is the generalization claim?\n\nRight now your experimental design is: **same task, same setup, same objects, less training data.** The claim is about **data efficiency within a single task setup.**\n\nBut the force normalization question reveals a deeper design choice:\n\n**Option A — Within-task data efficiency only.** All evaluations use the exact same physical setup (same objects, same surfaces, same lighting). The claim: \"instrumentation pretraining reaches 90% success with 25% of the data.\" Simple, clean, defensible.\n\n**Option B — Cross-condition generalization.** After proving data efficiency, add a generalization test: change the wiping surface material, change the bottle, etc. The claim: \"instrumentation pretraining not only needs less data but also generalizes better.\" Stronger claim, more experimental work.\n\n**My recommendation: Option A for the paper.** Keep it simple. Data efficiency under controlled conditions is already a strong contribution. Generalization can be mentioned as future work. If you try to claim both, you double the number of experiments and give reviewers twice as many angles to attack.\n\nDoes Option A align with what your promotor expects? Or is there pressure to show generalization too?\n\nAnd — park the ziplock instrumentation for now. You'll sort that with your promotor. In the meantime, you have **3 concrete tasks** (plugs, bottle, wiping) that can move forward."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":20429,"output":780,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":21209,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782814550369,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a8a5730c43409dcf"}}
{"type":"message","id":"583d8cc9","parentId":"7c0b21f9","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:30:04.661Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"for the normalize to percentile within the demonstration dataset looks intereseting because as you said the model learns is the force high or low relative to what's typical for this taks and if in my demonstration dataset I show that wood needs on average more force than glass, the model will also learn this. But remons me that I will need to talk to my promotor about what if the materials are similar and visually identical, then the model will have a hard time knowing what force to use, but maybe through the audio it will know. I will need to check how suitable different slideing forces are captured in the audio and also what force tollerance we have for this task, if we can simply always press at 5 N and get a good enough result then we don't need imitation learning at all and the task is not suitable. for the generalization claim, we will not do that for now, but remind me later that I need to talk about this to my promotor."}],"timestamp":1782815404660}}
{"type":"message","id":"e0879536","parentId":"583d8cc9","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:30:18.707Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to:\n1. Note the reminder about visually identical materials and force selection\n2. Note the reminder about discussing generalization with promotor\n3. Note the insight about force tolerance - if the task is too forgiving (any force works), it's not a good research task\n\nLet me also note that the user is thinking critically about whether the wiping task is even suitable for the research. Good scientific thinking.\n\nNow, let me move to the next question. What's the next logical branch in the decision tree?\n\nWe've covered:\n- What instrumentation means (task-specific auxiliary sensors)\n- How privileged info is fed into encoders (pretraining)\n\n\n- Same data problem (option B - two-stage pretrain then finetune)\n- Modality selection (suitability metric)\n- Instrumentation for each task\n- Generalization claim (within-task only for now)\n\nNext topics to cover:\n- Task ordering and complexity\n- Number of evaluation rollouts\n- How tasks are scripted vs. learned\n- The exact experimental protocol (data collection → pretraining → fine-tuning → evaluation)\n- How many episodes to collect before plateau\n- What \"reducing training data\" means exactly (random subsample? uniform across trajectories?)\n\nI think the next natural question is about the evaluation rollouts since the user asked this explicitly (\"how many are academically acceptable in robotics?\").\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNoted — I'll flag both reminders:\n\n- **🔶 REMINDER:** Visually identical materials → can the model disambiguate force via audio? Check suitability of sliding friction sounds across materials. Also check: what's the acceptable force tolerance for wiping? If any force in [2N, 8N] works equally well → task is too easy for IL research.\n- **🔶 REMINDER:** Discuss generalization claim with promotor (within-task only vs. cross-condition).\n\n---\n\n## Question 7: How many real-world evaluation rollouts per configuration?\n\nYou asked this upfront and it's a concrete number we need to lock in. This determines statistical power and experimental runtime.\n\nIn robotics literature (CoRL, ICRA, ICLR robotics tracks), typical ranges are:\n\n| Venue / Paper style | Rollouts per config |\n|---|---|\n| Minimum defensible | 20 |\n| Common standard | 30 |\n| Strong statistical backing | 50 |\n| Overkill / simulation | 100+ |\n\n**The calculation:** With 4 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels (100%, 75%, 50%, 25%) = **48 configurations**. At 30 rollouts each = **1,440 real-world rollouts**. At 50 = **2,400**.\n\nIf each rollout takes ~2 minutes (reset + execute + judge) = 48-80 hours of robot time per task, or ~4 days of continuous robot operation per task.\n\n**My recommendation: 30 rollouts per configuration.** This is the standard that reviewers accept without complaint. It gives you ~95% confidence intervals of roughly ±10% on success rates, which is enough to detect meaningful differences between variants. If a variant goes from 80% to 92%, 30 rollouts will show that as statistically significant.\n\n**But here's the catch:** Are rollouts fully automated (robot resets itself) or do you need manual resets? If manual, 1,440 rollouts at 3 min each = 72 hours of human labor. That's 2 full work weeks for one person.\n\nWhat's your reset situation — can the robot auto-reset between rollouts, or is it manual? And does 30 rollouts feel feasible given your timeline?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":21417,"output":809,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":22226,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782815404663,"responseId":"chatcmpl-920e492625228e1f"}}
{"type":"message","id":"db9a6ca3","parentId":"e0879536","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:37:36.047Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I would say 20 rollouts per config, and depending on the task, for example opening the bottle, this can be automatically reset, but for example the ziplockbag needs manual reset, so I would not count on automatic resets for every task."}],"timestamp":1782815856046}}
{"type":"message","id":"32fc1904","parentId":"db9a6ca3","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:37:48.988Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user settles on 20 rollouts per config. Let me calculate:\n\n4 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels = 48 configs × 20 rollouts = 960 rollouts total.\n\nAt ~3 min each (including manual reset), that's 48 hours of human labor. About 1 work week. More feasible.\n\nBut wait - the user said 4 tasks but ziplock bag instrumentation is still TBD. So realistically 3 tasks for now = 36 configs × 20 = 720 rollouts. About 36 hours. ~1 work week. More manageable.\n\nNow, what's the next question? We've covered:\n- Instrumentation definition\n- Encoder pretraining mechanism\n- Same data concern (option B)\n- Modality selection\n\n\n- Instrumentation for each task\n- Generalization claim\n- Number of rollouts\n\nI need to address how the training data gets reduced across those 4 levels. The approach here is critical—whether we're randomly sampling episodes, selecting specific ones, or using a different strategy entirely, since this choice directly impacts how the results should be interpreted.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nOK, locked in: **20 rollouts per configuration**. That's 48 configs × 20 = **960 rollouts** (~48 hours of hands-on time). Feasible.\n\n---\n\n## Question 8: How exactly do you reduce the training data?\n\nYou said 100%, 75%, 50%, 25%. But the *method* of reduction matters a lot for what your results mean. There are several approaches:\n\n**Option A — Random subsampling of episodes.** From 100 collected episodes, randomly pick 75 / 50 / 25. Simple, but a single random split might be lucky/unlucky. Usually repeated k times and averaged.\n\n**Option B — Curriculum by difficulty.** Remove the easiest or hardest episodes first. This tests whether instrumentation helps with hard or easy cases specifically.\n\n**Option C — Uniform prefix (first N episodes).** Use the first 75, first 50, first 25 episodes collected. Simplest to explain but assumes episodes are i.i.d., which they might not be (early episodes may be lower quality teleop).\n\n**Option D — Diverse subsampling.** Ensure the reduced set still covers the full diversity of demonstrations (e.g., different starting positions, different speeds). Most principled but also most complex to implement and justify.\n\n**My recommendation: Option A with k=5 repeats.** Randomly subsample 5 times at each reduction level, train 5 policies, average results. This:\n- Is simple to explain (\"we randomly sampled X% of episodes, repeated 5 times\")\n- Gives error bars across repeats\n- Avoids bias from ordering or hand-picking\n- Is standard practice in data efficiency literature\n\nHowever, this multiplies your training runs: 4 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels × 5 repeats = **240 policy trainings**. At maybe 2-4 hours each on GPU = 480-960 GPU hours. Feasible but not trivial.\n\n**Alternative if GPU budget is tight:** Option A with k=3 repeats, or even k=1 with a fixed random seed (report the seed for reproducibility). k=1 is defensible if you show the full 100% results are robust and the trend across reduction levels is clear.\n\nWhat's your GPU budget looking like, and does k=5 repeats feel feasible or should we reduce?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":22289,"output":766,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":23055,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782815856048,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bc0528c6a11788bd"}}
