{"type":"message","id":"3c134f94","parentId":"5dd319c7","timestamp":"2026-06-30T10:40:09.512Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user chooses Option C (uniform prefix, first N episodes) and assumes i.i.d. episodes. They don't want to multiply evaluation times by k.\n\nThis is a reasonable choice. Let me note it and move on.\n\nBut I should flag one concern: if episodes are truly i.i.d., then the order doesn't matter, and the first N episodes are as good as any random N episodes. But in practice, early teleop episodes tend to be lower quality (operator learning curve). This is a weakness of Option C that reviewers might flag.\n\nHowever, the user has made their choice and I should respect it. I'll note the concern briefly and move on.\n\nNow, what's the next question? We've covered:\n- Instrumentation definition ✓\n- Encoder pretraining mechanism ✓\n- Same data concern (option B) ✓\n\n\n- Modality selection ✓\n- Instrumentation for each task ✓\n- Generalization claim ✓\n- Number of rollouts ✓\n- Data reduction method ✓\n\nNext logical topic: How do we define and measure \"success\" for each task, and what other metrics matter? Success criteria need to be unambiguous and measurable for real-world evaluation, especially with the instrumentation sensors available.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nLocked in: **Option C — first N episodes (uniform prefix), assuming i.i.d.** Simple, no multiplication of runs. I'll flag for later: reviewers might question whether early episodes differ in quality from later ones (teleop learning curve). You can mitigate this by shuffling episodes before taking the prefix — gives you Option C's simplicity with Option A's randomness, at zero extra cost. Just sort with a fixed seed, then take first N.\n\n---\n\n## Question 9: How do you define \"success\" for each task?\n\nThis is the most critical evaluation detail. Success criteria must be:\n- **Unambiguous** (no subjective