{"type":"message","id":"ae1cae8a","parentId":"c267208c","timestamp":"2026-06-30T11:30:23.496Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user raises an important point about scripted vs. teleoperated demonstrations. Let me address this:\n\n1. Success criteria - aligned for all tasks, using instrumentation signals\n2. Scripted vs. teleoperated demonstrations - this is a new branch to explore\n\nThe user notes that some tasks (deformables like ziplock bag) are difficult to script, and they need consistency across tasks. This is a real design decision.\n\nLet me move to this question about scripted vs. teleoperated demonstrations.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nExcellent — instrumentation as the success signal for all tasks is a very clean design. No separate evaluation logic needed. The same sensor that supervises training also defines success at test time.\n\n---\n\n## Question 10: Scripted vs. teleoperated demonstrations — which approach?\n\nThis is a fundamental design choice with trade-offs:\n\n**Teleoperated demonstrations** (e.g., via spacemouse or Gello):\n- Natural human behavior, variable trajectories\n- Captures the \"how would a human do this?\" policy\n- More diverse data (different speeds, angles, approaches)\n- Higher noise, some bad episodes\n- Harder to reproduce exactly\n\n**Scripted demonstrations** (programmed trajectories):\n- Precise, repeatable, clean data\n- Can control exactly what the robot does (force profiles, trajectories)\n- Less diversity — all demos follow similar paths\n- May not capture the full state space a learned policy might explore\n- Easier to argue about data quality\n\n**The tension you identified:** Deformable objects (ziplock bag) are hard to script because the dynamics change with every interaction. A scripted trajectory that works once may fail the next time the bag shifts. For rigid objects (plugs, bottles), scripting is straightforward.\n\n**My recommendation: Teleoperated demonstrations for all tasks.** Here's why:\n\n1. **Consistency:** All tasks use the same data collection method. No reviewer can say \"scripted demos are cleaner, that's why it worked.\"\n2. **Ecological validity:** The policy learns from real human behavior, not from a perfect trajectory. This is more realistic for the imitation learning setting.\n3. **Diversity:** Teleop naturally produces trajectory diversity, which is important for robust policies.\n4. **The deformable argument:** If you script rigid tasks but teleop deformable tasks, you're confounding task type with data collection method. Teleop everywhere avoids this.\n\n**For the wiping task specifically:** Teleoperation lets the demonstrator apply natural variable force. The load cell records what force the human actually used. The policy learns from that distribution. At test time, success is defined relative to that same distribution. Clean.\n\n**The counterargument against teleop:** More bad episodes to filter. You'll need a success filter on demonstrations (only use successful episodes for training). This is standard practice anyway.\n\nWhat's your take — does teleop for all tasks work for you, or is there pressure from your promotor to script some tasks?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":24146,"output":629,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":24775,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782819012477,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8e02d00444127d03"}}
