{"type":"message","id":"3b3c3694","parentId":"86a94dab","timestamp":"2026-06-30T11:35:45.419Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user raises a very important point. If the teleoperator doesn't receive force feedback, then the force data collected from the load cell during demonstrations is just a passive recording of what force the teleoperator happened to apply — not what force is *intentionally* being targeted. \n\nThis means:\n1. The teleoperator is controlling position/velocity, not force\n2. The force varies unintentionally based on how the teleoperator moves\n3. The \"expert\" force profile may not be expert at all — it's just whatever force results from position control\n\nThis is a real concern. Let me think about the implications:\n\nFor the wiping task specifically:\n- If the teleoperator doesn't feel force, they can't intentionally apply \"just right\" pressure\n\n\n- The resulting force is a byproduct of the trajectory, not a controlled variable\n- Pretraining the audio encoder to predict this \"expert force\" is pretraining on noise, not on meaningful supervision\n\nBut there's a silver lining: if the audio signal captures friction sounds that correlate with force, then pretraining on force labels teaches the audio encoder to recognize what \"appropriate contact\" sounds like, even if the teleoperator couldn't control force directly. The real question is whether the teleoperator can visually gauge appropriate pressure or if there's a way to provide feedback during demonstration collection.\n\nI'm considering options like showing a real-time force gauge on screen so the teleoperator has a target range, which would make the force data meaningful supervision. Or I could reframe the wiping task to rely on audio feedback instead—having the teleoperator listen for the sound of proper contact pressure. But this raises a deeper issue about what the policy is actually learning: if force isn't part of the observation or action space, the encoder would be pretraining on information the policy can never actually use, which defeats the purpose of the representation learning.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThis is a sharp observation and it exposes a real problem. Let me unpack it.\n\n**The core issue:** If you're teleoperating via position control (spacemouse/Gello) without haptic feedback, the force is a *byproduct* of your trajectory, not an intentional control signal. The \"expert\" force profile is just whatever force happened when you moved the arm — it's not expert force, it's *incidental* force.\n\nThis has two implications:\n\n**1. The policy may not have access to force anyway.** If your policy observations are [image, audio spectrogram, robot state], then force isn't an input. The encoder pretraining on force labels teaches the encoder to predict something the policy doesn't see. The learned representations might still help (force correlates with visual contact features), but the link is indirect.\n\n**2. The quality of supervision depends on intentionality.** If the teleoperator can't feel or see the force, the force signal is noisy and inconsistent across episodes. One demo might press at 3N, another at 7N, not because of strategy but because of teleop variance.\n\n**My recommendation: Give the teleoperator force feedback during data collection.**\n\nAdd a simple **real-time force visualization** (a bar or needle on screen) during teleoperation. Set a target range (e.g., 4-6N for wiping). The teleoperator adjusts their motion to keep the needle in range. Now the force data represents *intentional* expert behavior.\n\nThis is low effort: you already have `ipc_ft.py` and load cell reading code. Just plot it with a target band during teleop.\n\n**Alternative if you can't add feedback:** Use **audio as the primary signal** for wiping, not force. The click/scrub sound of proper contact is audible. Give the teleoperator audio feedback (through headphones) and ask them to \"wipe until it sounds right.\" The instrumentation (load cell) still provides the ground truth label for pretraining, but the teleoperator's control channel is audio, which matches the encoder you're pretraining.\n\nWhich approach appeals more — adding visual force feedback to teleop, or shifting the wiping task to be audio-driven?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":24820,"output":850,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":25670,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782819330441,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8b0f6066d86aeb09"}}
