{"type":"message","id":"7d3ae33c","parentId":"461c8b00","timestamp":"2026-06-30T11:38:24.319Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user has decided on visual force feedback during teleop. Good, clean decision. Let me lock that in and move to the next question.\n\nWhat have we covered:\n- Instrumentation definition ✓\n- Encoder pretraining mechanism ✓\n- Same data concern (option B) ✓\n- Modality selection ✓\n- Instrumentation for each task ✓\n- Generalization claim ✓\n- Number of rollouts ✓\n- Data reduction method ✓\n- Success criteria ✓\n- Scripted vs. teleop ✓\n- Force feedback during teleop ✓\n\nWhat's left:\n- Task ordering (simple → complex, one-arm → bimanual)\n\n\n- Experimental timeline and milestones\n- Which specific metrics beyond success rate should I track\n- How the three policy variants are actually compared (statistical tests?)\n- Baseline comparisons and evaluation rigor\n- Potential limitations and how to address them\n\nThe user mentioned wanting to start with simpler tasks and progress to more difficult ones, plus moving from one-arm to bimanual setups. I should clarify which tasks fit into which complexity category and how that affects the experimental design.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nLocked in: **Visual force feedback during teleop.** Real-time bar/needle with target range. Makes force data intentional and meaningful.\n\n---\n\n## Question 11: Task ordering — which tasks are simple vs. complex, one-arm vs. bimanual?\n\nYou mentioned wanting to work from simple to complex, one-arm first then bimanual. Let me map your tasks:\n\n| Task | Arms needed | Complexity | Why? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rubber plugs** | One | Low | Simple insertion motion, clear success signal, rigid objects |\n| **Bottle opening** | One (or two?) | Medium | Requires rotation + force, tactile feedback important |\n| **Ziplock bag** | One (or two?) | Medium-High | Deformable object, unpredictable