{"type":"message","id":"a61766e8","parentId":"02bea6ae","timestamp":"2026-06-30T12:05:18.200Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking me to recap what we decided about pretraining vs. auxiliary loss. Let me remember:\n\n- We discussed Option A (auxiliary loss during policy training) vs Option B (two-stage pretrain-then-finetune)\n- The user chose Option B\n- The rationale: you can immediately see if the modality is suitable for predicting privileged information\n\nThe user's senior suggests training until target loss (~0.001) for from-scratch, then using that step count for other variants, per task.\n\nLet me think about this. The senior's approach is actually smarter than fixed 100K steps because:\n1. Different tasks need different amounts of training\n2. Binary classification tasks (plugs) might converge faster than continuous regression tasks (wiping)\n3. Using a target loss ensures all variants are trained \"equally well\" in terms of optimization progress\n\n\n4. But 0.001 is a very specific target that may not generalize across all tasks and loss functions\n\nThe real comparison is between training each variant to the same convergence point versus training them for the same number of steps. The senior's approach—matching step counts to when from-scratch reaches the target loss—is actually the fairest way to compare, since it gives pretrained variants the same optimization budget as the baseline.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: the pretrained variant might converge faster and reach a lower loss, so using the from-scratch step count could leave it undertrained. Still, I think this approach is solid because it isolates whether pretraining actually helps with data efficiency—if the pretrained variant achieves better performance with the same training budget and less data, that's a genuine advantage rather than an artifact of extended training.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nQuick reca