{"type":"message","id":"b9e6c54a","parentId":"a72d112f","timestamp":"2026-06-30T12:49:31.944Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user confirmed. Let me lock this in and think about what's left.\n\nWe've covered a comprehensive set of decisions. Let me review the remaining open questions:\n\n1. Statistical significance testing - how to compare variants\n2. What if suitability scores are tied between modalities?\n3. Any other overlooked aspects?\n\nActually, let me think about whether there are more substantive questions or if we should wrap up with a summary. The user asked me to \"interview relentlessly until we reach shared understanding.\" I think we're close to that point.\n\nLet me ask one more important question: how do you handle the case where the suitability metric shows BOTH modalities are poor at predicting the instrumentation signal? This would mean the instrumentation doesn't translate well to either image or audio features, and the task might not be suitable for the research.\n\nActually, I think there's one more practical question: the pretraining dataset. The user said pretraining data = demonstration data. But with demo filtering (successful only), and data reduction (25%, 50%, 75%), the pretraining data for the instrumentation variant at 25% is only 25% of demos. Does the pretraining also use the reduced set, or does it use the full N?\n\n\nThis is actually a critical design decision. If pretraining uses the full dataset while policy training uses reduced data, then the pretrained variant always gets more training signal than from-scratch, which could unfairly favor it. But if pretraining also uses only the reduced set, then at 25% the encoder has very little to learn from, which might be the point of the experiment.\n\nI think pretraining should use the same reduced subset for a fair comparison — this tests whether instrumentation pretraining helps even when both stages are data-constrained.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nLocked in: **Successful demos only. Failed episodes discarded during collection.**\n\n---\n\n## Question 19: Pretraining data — full N or the reduced subset?\n\nAt the 25% data level, you have 25% of N successful demonstrations. For the instrumentation-pretrained variant:\n\n**Option A — Pretrain on full N, finetune on 25%.** The encoder sees all the data during pretraining, but the policy is trained on only 25%. The encoder already learned great representations from the full dataset.\n\n**Option B — Pretrain on 25%, finetune on 25%.** Both stages see only the reduced subset. The encoder must learn from limited data too.\n\n**The problem with Option A:** It's not really data efficiency — the encoder saw all the data during pretraining. You're comparing \"encoder trained on 100% + policy trained on 25%\" vs. \"everything trained on 25%\". The pretrained variant got 125% of the training signal vs. 25%. That's confounded.\n\n**Option B is the honest data efficiency test:** Both pretraining and fine-tuning see only the reduced subset. If the instrumentation variant still outperforms from-scratch at 25%, it proves that *the instrumentation signal is more informative per sample*, not that seeing more data helps.\n\n**My recommendation: Option B — pretrain and finetune on the same reduced subset.** This is the stricter, more credible test of data efficiency. The claim becomes: \"even when both pretraining and policy learning are constrained to 25% of data, instrumentation supervision yields better policies.\"\n\nDoes Option B work for you?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":34011,"output":736,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":34747,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782823759153,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9dbabbfd2c4ae834"}}
