{"type":"message","id":"0d71169a","parentId":"116944dd","timestamp":"2026-06-30T12:50:40.994Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user confirmed Option B. Let me lock this in.\n\nWe've covered almost everything now. Let me think about what's remaining:\n\n1. Statistical significance - how to compare variants\n2. What if suitability scores are tied?\n3. Summary of all decisions\n\nI think we're close to wrapping up. Let me ask about statistical significance, then provide a comprehensive summary.\n\nActually, let me think about whether there's one more important design question. The user mentioned \"other stuff that I have overlooked.\" Let me think about what could be overlooked:\n\n- Random seeds for reproducibility\n- Hyperparameter tuning - do all variants use the same hyperparameters? What if the optimal LR differs per variant?\n- The evaluation protocol - are rollouts done in a fixed order or randomized?\n- Timeout per rollout - what if the policy gets stuck?\n- The pretraining architecture - is it the same encoder architecture as used in the policy, or a separate one?\n\n\n- For a clean paper, I should use the same hyperparameters across all variants to keep things simple and defensible, fixing random seeds for reproducibility, implementing rollouts with timeouts to prevent the policy from getting stuck, and using the identical encoder architecture for both pretraining and policy training. I'll ask about statistical significance and timeout handling, then wrap up with a comprehensive summary.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nLocked in: **Both pretraining and fine-tuning use the same reduced subset.** Honest data efficiency test.\n\n---\n\n## Question 20: Rollout timeout and statistical significance — the last operational details\n\n**Timeout per rollout:** What happens if the policy gets stuck in a loop and never finishes? You need a hard timeout.\n\n**My recommendation:** Set timeout to **2× the median demonstration duration** for each task. If the policy hasn't succeeded by then, mark as failed. Record the timeout. This prevents the robot from running forever on a broken policy.\n\n**Statistical significance:** With 20 rollouts, you get success rates as proportions. How do you claim \"variant A is better than variant B\"?\n\n**My recommendation:** **Binomial confidence intervals (95%)** for each success rate, and **Fisher's exact test** for pairwise comparisons between variants at each data level. This is standard for small-sample proportion comparisons. Report p-values in the paper. If 95% CIs don't overlap, the difference is statistically significant.\n\nExample: At 25% data, instrumentation-pretrained = 65% success [47-80%], from-scratch = 25% success [11-47%], p = 0.012 → significant.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of the Full Experimental Design\n\nLet me compile everything:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_560d0ded96884ba7935d8441","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat << 'EXPERIMENT'\n=================================================================\nEXPERIMENTAL DESIGN: Instrumentation for Data-Efficient Imitation Learning\n=================================================================\n\nRESEARCH QUESTION:\n  Does task-specific instrumentation (privileged sensor signals available\n  only during training) improve data-efficient policy learning?\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\n  Supervised pretraining on instrumentation labels teaches encoders to\n  focus on task-relevant features, requiring fewer demonstrations to\n  reach target performance vs. random initialization or generic pretraining.\n\nHARDWARE:\n  2× UR5 arms, Robotiq + Schunk grippers, camera, microphone, rerun viz\n\nTASKS (ordered by complexity):\n  1. Rubber plug insertion (one-arm, rigid, binary success)\n     Instrumentation: metal ring circuit closure\n     Modality: audio (spectrogram click detection)\n  \n  2. Bottle cap opening (one-arm, rigid+rotation, continuous success)\n     Instrumentation: LDR in cap\n     Modality: image (cap rotation visible)\n  \n  3. Disk wiping (one-arm, force control, continuous quality)\n     Instrumentation: load cell under wiping surface\n     Modality: audio (friction sound)\n  \n  4. Ziplock bag opening (TWO-ARM, deformable) — LATER if time allows\n     Instrumentation: TBD with promotor\n\nPOLICY ARCHITECTURE:\n  Diffusion Policy (lerobot), image + audio encoders, fine-tuned end-to-end\n\nTHREE VARIANTS PER TASK:\n  A. From scratch: random init for all encoders\n  B. Instrumentation pretrained: chosen encoder pretrained on instrumentation\n     labels (same reduced data subset), then fine-tuned\n  C. Generic pretrained: same encoder pretrained on ImageNet/AudioSet,\n     then fine-tuned\n  \n  Same hyperparameters, same training steps for all variants.\n\nDATA COLLECTION PROTOCOL:\n  - Teleoperation for all tasks (spacemouse/Gello)\n  - Visual force feedback during wiping teleop\n  - Successful episodes only (filtered by instrumentation signal)\n  - Iterative collection: batches of 20, train from-scratch, evaluate\n  - Stop when: 90% success achieved, OR plateau detected (<5% improvement\n    over 2 consecutive batches), OR cap of 100 episodes reached\n  - Let final count = N (task-dependent)\n  - Shuffle episodes with fixed seed, take prefix for reduction levels\n\nDATA REDUCTION LEVELS:\n  100%, 75%, 50%, 25% of N (first episodes after shuffle)\n\nEVALUATION:\n  - 20 real-world rollouts per (task × variant × data level)\n  - Success criterion: derived from instrumentation signal\n  - Rollout timeout: 2× median demonstration duration\n  - Primary metric: success rate (%)\n  - Statistical test: Fisher's exact test, 95% binomial CIs\n\nPRETRAINING PROTOCOL:\n  - Same reduced data subset as fine-tuning (honest data efficiency)\n  - Train until validation loss plateaus (early stopping, patience=500)\n  - BCE loss for binary (plugs), MSE for continuous (bottle, wiping)\n  - LR: 1e-4\n  - Encoder choice via suitability metric: pretrain both image and audio\n    on held-out 20% val split, pick modality with higher val accuracy/R²\n\nTOTAL EXPERIMENTAL SCALE (3 tasks):\n  - Configurations: 3 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels = 36\n  - Rollouts: 36 × 20 = 720 (~36 hours hands-on)\n  - Policy trainings: 36 (~72-144 GPU hours)\n  - Pretraining runs: 3 tasks × 2 modalities = 6 (negligible)\n\n=================================================================\nOPEN ITEMS / REMINDERS:\n=================================================================\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: ziplock bag instrumentation\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: generalization claim scope\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: visually identical materials + audio disambiguation\n🔶 Check: force tolerance for wiping (task suitability)\n🔶 Literature search: target loss values for IL policies\n🔶 Implement: visual force feedback during wiping teleop\n🔶 Implement: instrumentation hardware for all 3 tasks\n🔶 Implement: suitability metric pipeline (pretrain image vs audio, compare)\n=================================================================\nEXPERIMENT\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":34759,"output":1585,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":36344,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1782823814753,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bf386ed4be31670c"}}
