or all variants.\n\nDATA COLLECTION PROTOCOL:\n  - Teleoperation for all tasks (spacemouse/Gello)\n  - Visual force feedback during wiping teleop\n  - Successful episodes only (filtered by instrumentation signal)\n  - Iterative collection: batches of 20, train from-scratch, evaluate\n  - Stop when: 90% success achieved, OR plateau detected (<5% improvement\n    over 2 consecutive batches), OR cap of 100 episodes reached\n  - Let final count = N (task-dependent)\n  - Shuffle episodes with fixed seed, take prefix for reduction levels\n\nDATA REDUCTION LEVELS:\n  100%, 75%, 50%, 25% of N (first episodes after shuffle)\n\nEVALUATION:\n  - 20 real-world rollouts per (task × variant × data level)\n  - Success criterion: derived from instrumentation signal\n  - Rollout timeout: 2× median demonstration duration\n  - Primary metric: success rate (%)\n  - Statistical test: Fisher's exact test, 95% binomial CIs\n\nPRETRAINING PROTOCOL:\n  - Same reduced data subset as fine-tuning (honest data efficiency)\n  - Train until validation loss plateaus (early stopping, patience=500)\n  - BCE loss for binary (plugs), MSE for continuous (bottle, wiping)\n  - LR: 1e-4\n  - Encoder choice via suitability metric: pretrain both image and audio\n    on held-out 20% val split, pick modality with higher val accuracy/R²\n\nTOTAL EXPERIMENTAL SCALE (3 tasks):\n  - Configurations: 3 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels = 36\n  - Rollouts: 36 × 20 = 720 (~36 hours hands-on)\n  - Policy trainings: 36 (~72-144 GPU hours)\n  - Pretraining runs: 3 tasks × 2 modalities = 6 (negligible)\n\n=================================================================\nOPEN ITEMS / REMINDERS:\n=================================================================\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: ziplock bag instrumentation\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: generalization claim scope\n🔶 Discuss with promotor: visually identical materials + audio disambiguation\n🔶 Check: force tolerance for wiping (task suitability)\n🔶 Literature search: target loss values for IL policies\n🔶 Implement: visual force feedback during wiping teleop\n🔶 Implement: instrumentation hardware for all 3 tasks\n🔶 Implement: suitability metric pipeline (pretrain image vs audio, compare)\n=================================================================\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1782823841019}}
{"type":"message","id":"4bf6d49d","parentId":"4f119c53","timestamp":"2026-06-30T12:50:50.383Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"We've covered the full experimental design now. Let me do a final check — is there anything the user mentioned that we haven't addressed?\n\nUser's original statement:\n- ✅ Instrumentation helps improve policy performance (research question)\n- ✅ Train task, reduce training data, measure performance\n- ✅ Rollouts with success rate, time to complete, other metrics\n- ✅ 3 policy types: from scratch, instrumentation pretrained, generic pretrained\n- ✅ Performance improvement from instrumentation, not pretraining itself\n- ✅ Number of training episodes (iterative protocol)\n- ✅ Collect until 90% or plateau\n- ✅ Data reduction at 75%, 50%, 25%\n- ✅ Tasks: rubber plugs, bottle, ziplock, wiping\n- ✅ Need to know how to instrument each task\n- ✅ Need to know if scripting or not (decided: teleop)\n\n\n- ✅ Real world rollouts, how many academically acceptable (20)\n- ✅ Simple to complex, one-arm to bimanual\n- ✅ \"Maybe other stuff I have overlooked\"\n\nI think we've been thorough. The design is comprehensive. Let me give the user a closing summary and ask if there's anything they want to revisit or dig deeper into.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nWe've walked through 20 questions covering the full experimental design. Here's where we stand:\n\n**The design is locked.** The summary above captures every decision we made. The experimental protocol is simple enough to explain in a single paragraph:\n\n> *\"For each of 3 manipulation tasks, we equip the environment with a task-specific sensor providing privileged information during training only. We pretrain either the image or audio encoder to predict this signal, then fine-tune the full policy. We compare against random initialization and generic pretraining (ImageNet/AudioSet) across 4 data reduction levels (100%, 75%, 50%, 25%), evaluating with 20 real-world rollouts each. The instrumentation-pretrained encoder consistently achieves higher success rates with fewer demonstrations, proving that task-specific instrumentation — not pretraining in general — drives data efficiency.\"*\n\n**Next concrete steps:**\n1. Resolve the 🔶 items with your promotor\n2. Build instrumentation hardware for plugs, bottle, wiping\n3. Implement suitability metric pipeline\n4. Start with rubber plugs (simplest) to validate the full pipeline\n\nIs there any branch of the design tree you want me to grill you on further, or are you satisfied with the experimental design?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":37335,"output":525,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":37860,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1782823841022,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a1f190e8ecec6dfb"}}
{"type":"message","id":"e13504d8","parentId":"4bf6d49d","timestamp":"2026-07-08T10:06:32.553Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have talked about this with my promotor. there is a Promotor_Meeting_Report.pdf on my desktop and also a markdown or html version somewhere else, you can read it this is what I used to guide the meeting I will now tell you what the conclusions were. for the ziplockbag we will postpone the experiment, a potential way to instrument it is using button like square pads, disconnected so not one continuous strip. the number of pads that are close to eachother and make a connection the more the ziplocbag is closed, but again this is more for the long term. Then experiment 1 the plug. I talked about the generalisation with my promotor. THe conclusion was that eventhough generalisation si not our main focus, we should show some form of generalisation because otherwhise there is no benefit of imitation learning at all, the reason is because every single task can be scripted, it is the generalisation where the scripted method fails. So to show generalisation with the plug, it does not have to be very advanced, but we were thinking about for example having 6 different sizes of plugs, then training with plugs with size 1 3 5 and testing with plugs 1 2 3 4 5 6, however right now I'm thinking that then we are mixing the question of instrumentation improves generalisation with the question if instrumentation improves data efficiency. It would be maybe better if we could test once in distribution and once out of distribution, but that would double our rollouts? or no even better I do 10 evaluation rollouts in distribution and 10 evaluation rollouts out of distrubition, that gives me 20 rollouts in total. Then we would do the experiment with the plugs cluttered in a box together, similar to how it is done in industry. and for instrumentation we were thinking instead of putting metal ring on plug that it would be better to instrument the metal sheet itself, for example some button like construction in the metal sheer so whem the plug sits firmly then we can read this out as a button that is bein pressed. For the bottle opening experiment, we were thinking about variation in terms of different bottles that we will 3d print, however the opening mechanism with the spring is something i'm not confident enough about that I can design it. for the LDR instrumentation is nuce but probably we will use a phototransistor. and we can also use multiple phototransistors. Then we have a new experiment of getting ziplock bags with items out of the bottle, but this is also something that will be postponed for the long term. Then we have the disk, it is actually a petri dish with agar. there was also a misunderstanding, instead of wiping the task with the petri dish is actually pressing in a rolling motion over different objects ( objects can be flat or round or anything). The idea can be giving it also some coordinates to the policy as input so the poicy knows where to push the petri dish. Then gor generalisation we could use 6 different surfaces and force is indeed very important here. The usecase is actually that people have the petri dish, they have a location on an abject, they dgo to the object, open the petri dish, they do the rolling motion and they close the petri dish, instrumentation force based is indeed interesting and my doubds about instrumentation that is also available in the internal FT sensor is something we don't have to think about right now. according to my promotor it is not precise enough. Then we also have a new task which is wiping, so after the petri disk has rolled over a point on a surface we need to wipe the aread with a cleaning cloth. like a tissue out of a box. this wiping is in an s like motion and also force here is important for instrumentation we were thinking about holding a camera over an area, the camera analyzes image feed and it holds like a progress value with it, for example the are is divided into a grid of points 5 cm apart and we need to go over all points with the gripper in the end. the camera feed can tell us the index we are currently at and then the poilicy should learn to go to the next index. like hitting beavers game with a hammer but the beavers are sequential and in an s wiping motion. so vision based instrumentation has potential here. Then we have an additional task of company abc with casting pieces, when putting a piece inplace it could be that it doesn't fit perfectly. for example mark rober once made a vide where he made a white puzzle and sometimes it could be that the puzzle piece would not fit perfectly after laying it. he solved it through ticking 3 times on the piece so it fell into place, thowever with casting pieces this is not possible. THe idea here would be that we want the pieces to fit perfectly and to do this we can instrument for example witha distance sensor or ultrasonic sensor or camera based. That was is for what we discussed in the meeting. Now for the next meeting my promotor wants per task in detail as far as i can go, formalize and refine everything. and finally think about the figures and tables in the paper and the story and message."}],"timestamp":1783505192549}}
