{"type":"message","id":"db6bc644","parentId":"5fddfb60","timestamp":"2026-07-08T10:06:50.581Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_cf637f59ed384946a26af51f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1400 866\" font-family=\"'Segoe UI',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif\"><defs><marker id=\"arw\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\" markerUnits=\"strokeWidth\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1400\" height=\"866\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/><rect x=\"28\" y=\"20\" width=\"1344\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"52\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"24\" font-weight=\"700\">Instrumented Imitation Learning — Decision Map</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"76\" fill=\"#c7d2fe\" font-size=\"14.5\">Hypothesis: supervising an encoder with task instrumentation (privileged, training-only) yields better data efficiency than random or generic pretraining.</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\">EXPERIMENT PIPELINE</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M28,160 L28,139 Q28,130 37,130 L223,130 Q232,130 232,139 L232,160 Z\" fill=\"#0f766e\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">1  Collect data</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Teleop, all tasks</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• + real-time visual force</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">   feedback</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"232.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Iterative: batches of 20</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Stop at 90% or plateau;</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"267.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">   cap 100</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"285\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Successful demos only</text><line x1=\"234\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"254\" y2=\"237\" stroke=\"#1e3a8a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"256\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M256,160 L256,139 Q256,130 265,130 L451,130 Q460,130 460,139 L460,160 Z\" fill=\"#0f766e\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">2  Reduce data</text><text x=\"268\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• First-N prefix (uniform)</text><text x=\"268\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• 100 / 75 / 50 / 25%</text><text x=\"268\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Assume episodes i.i.d.</text><line x1=\"462\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"482\" y2=\"237\" stroke=\"#1e3a8a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"484\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M484,160 L484,139 Q484,130 493,130 L679,130 Q688,130 688,139 L688,160 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"496\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">3  Suitability test</text><text x=\"496\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Pretrain image vs audio</text><text x=\"496\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• to predict instrumentation</text><text x=\"496\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Pick higher val score</text><text x=\"496\" y=\"232.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• = picks the modality</text><line x1=\"690\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"710\" y2=\"237\" stroke=\"#1e3a8a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"712\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M712,160 L712,139 Q712,130 721,130 L907,130 Q916,130 916,139 L916,160 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"724\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">4  Three variants (init)</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• From scratch (random)</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Instrumentation-pretrained</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Generic (ImageNet /</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"232.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">   AudioSet)</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• All encoders fine-tuned</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"267.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Same reduced subset</text><line x1=\"918\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"938\" y2=\"237\" stroke=\"#1e3a8a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"940\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M940,160 L940,139 Q940,130 949,130 L1135,130 Q1144,130 1144,139 L1144,160 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"952\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">5  Train policy</text><text x=\"952\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Diffusion Policy (lerobot)</text><text x=\"952\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Fixed step budget (~100k)</text><text x=\"952\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Select by rollout success,</text><text x=\"952\" y=\"232.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• NOT training loss</text><line x1=\"1146\" y1=\"237\" x2=\"1166\" y2=\"237\" stroke=\"#1e3a8a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" marker-end=\"url(#arw)\"/><rect x=\"1168\" y=\"130\" width=\"204\" height=\"214\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M1168,160 L1168,139 Q1168,130 1177,130 L1363,130 Q1372,130 1372,139 L1372,160 Z\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\"/><text x=\"1180\" y=\"151\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">6  Evaluate</text><text x=\"1180\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• 20 real rollouts / config</text><text x=\"1180\" y=\"197.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Success rate = primary</text><text x=\"1180\" y=\"215\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Binomial CI + Fisher test</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"372\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\">TASKS &amp; INSTRUMENTATION</text><text x=\"298\" y=\"372\" fill=\"#6b7280\" font-size=\"12.7\">Order 1→2→3 first (single-arm) · instrumentation doubles as the success signal</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"386\" width=\"322.5\" height=\"176\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M28,416 L28,395 Q28,386 37,386 L341.5,386 Q350.5,386 350.5,395 L350.5,416 Z\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"407\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">1. Rubber plugs</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"436\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Arms: 1</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"453.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Sensor: circuit closer (binary)</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"471\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Modality (hyp.): audio</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"488.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Success: circuit closed</text><rect x=\"368.5\" y=\"386\" width=\"322.5\" height=\"176\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M368.5,416 L368.5,395 Q368.5,386 377.5,386 L682,386 Q691,386 691,395 L691,416 Z\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/><text x=\"380.5\" y=\"407\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">2. Bottle opening</text><text x=\"380.5\" y=\"436\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Arms: 1</text><text x=\"380.5\" y=\"453.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Sensor: LDR in cap (continuous)</text><text x=\"380.5\" y=\"471\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Modality (hyp.): image</text><text x=\"380.5\" y=\"488.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Success: LDR &gt; threshold</text><rect x=\"709\" y=\"386\" width=\"322.5\" height=\"176\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M709,416 L709,395 Q709,386 718,386 L1022.5,386 Q1031.5,386 1031.5,395 L1031.5,416 Z\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\"/><text x=\"721\" y=\"407\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">3. Disk wiping</text><text x=\"721\" y=\"436\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Arms: 1</text><text x=\"721\" y=\"453.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Sensor: load cell (continuous)</text><text x=\"721\" y=\"471\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Modality (hyp.): audio</text><text x=\"721\" y=\"488.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Success: force in range</text><rect x=\"1049.5\" y=\"386\" width=\"322.5\" height=\"176\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f9fafb\" stroke=\"#9ca3af\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><path d=\"M1049.5,416 L1049.5,395 Q1049.5,386 1058.5,386 L1363,386 Q1372,386 1372,395 L1372,416 Z\" fill=\"#6b7280\"/><text x=\"1061.5\" y=\"407\" fill=\"#ffffff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">4. Ziplock bag</text><text x=\"1061.5\" y=\"436\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Arms: 2 — bimanual</text><text x=\"1061.5\" y=\"453.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• DEFERRED (if time allows)</text><text x=\"1061.5\" y=\"471\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Sensor: TBD · Modality: image</text><text x=\"1061.5\" y=\"488.5\" fill=\"#1f2937\" font-size=\"12.7\">• Success: slider fully open</text><rect x=\"28\" y=\"588\" width=\"1344\" height=\"254\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fffbeb\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><rect x=\"28\" y=\"588\" width=\"1344\" height=\"30\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f59e0b\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"609\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14.5\" font-weight=\"700\">OPEN QUESTIONS FOR PROMOTOR  (report §8)</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"640\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"13.3\" font-weight=\"700\">1. Ziplock sensor</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"658\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">Hall-effect + magnet? string pot? — decide the</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"675\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">instrumentation.</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"700\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"13.3\" font-weight=\"700\">2. Wiping validity</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"718\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">Can audio disambiguate look-alike materials? Is there a</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"735\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">real, narrow force tolerance (else task too easy)?</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"760\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"13.3\" font-weight=\"700\">3. Generalization claim</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"778\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">Within-task data efficiency only, or add cross-condition</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"795\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">generalization?</text><text x=\"710\" y=\"640\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"13.3\" font-weight=\"700\">4. Demonstrations</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"658\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">Confirm teleoperation for ALL tasks (no scripting, to avoid</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"675\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">confound).</text><text x=\"710\" y=\"700\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"13.3\" font-weight=\"700\">5. Training duration</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"718\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">Fixed step budget + select by rollout success — align with</text><text x=\"724\" y=\"735\" fill=\"#4b5563\" font-size=\"12.6\">senior (vs 0.001-loss idea).</text></svg>"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1783505210581}}
{"type":"message","id":"80a22cd5","parentId":"db6bc644","timestamp":"2026-07-08T10:07:38.584Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a much clearer picture. Let me summarize the key changes from the meeting:\n\n1. **Ziplock bag**: Postponed. Potential instrumentation: disconnected square pads that make connections as bag closes. But deferred.\n\n2. **Experiment 1 - Plugs**: \n   - Plugs cluttered in a box (industry-like setting)\n   - Instrumentation: instrument the metal sheet itself (button-like construction), not the plug\n   - Generalization: 6 plug sizes, train on 1,3,5, test on 1,2,3,4,5,6\n   - BUT user is concerned about mixing data efficiency + generalization questions\n   - User's idea: 10 in-distribution rollouts + 10 out-of-distribution rollouts = 20 total\n   \n3. **Bottle opening**: I'm planning to 3D print different bottle variations for testing, though I'm less certain about the spring mechanism design. I'll switch to phototransistors instead of LDRs and might use multiple sensors. There's also a new task involving removing ziplock bags with items from the bottle, but that's deferred for now.\n\n4. **Disk task**: This is actually about pressing a petri dish with agar in a rolling motion over various objects rather than wiping. I could provide coordinates to the policy so it knows where to position the petri dish, and I'm considering testing across 6 different surfaces since force control is critical. The full task sequence involves the person holding the petri dish, navigating to an object, opening it, performing the rolling motion, then closing it—with the internal force-torque sensor not being precise enough for this work.\n\n5. **Wiping task**: This is a separate new task that comes after the petri dish step. I'd use a cleaning cloth from a box in an S-shaped motion where force matters. I'm thinking about using vision-based instrumentation—a camera overhead tracking a grid of points 5cm apart, telling the policy which index to target next, like a whack-a-mole game.\n\n6. **Company ABC casting pieces**: For fitting pieces that don't seat perfectly, I could use distance sensors, ultrasonic sensors, or camera-based detection to ensure they fit properly.\n\nNow I need to formalize and refine each task in detail for the next meeting, then work on the paper's figures, tables, narrative, and overall message.\n\nThe key shift here is that generalization isn't optional anymore—the promotor wants to demonstrate it. And the task list has expanded significantly from what we originally discussed: plugs with multiple sizes in a cluttered box, bottle opening with variations, the petri dish rolling task across different surfaces, wiping as a separate S-motion task, and the casting pieces from Company ABC. I should focus on getting the plugs experiment detailed first since that's the immediate priority, while being mindful of the tension between showing generalization and keeping the instrumentation question clean. user was worried about mixing data efficiency + generalization. Their proposed solution: 10 ID + 10 OOD rollouts = 20 total. This is smart.\n\nLet me now help the user formalize everything. They need:\n1. Per-task detailed descriptions\n2. Figures and tables for the paper\n3. Story and message\n\nLet me organize this systematically.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nExcellent — this is a lot of new information. Let me digest everything and reorganize. The promotor meeting significantly changed the scope:\n\n**Key changes from our earlier design:**\n1. **Generalization is now required** — not optional. \"Every task can be scripted; IL's value is generalization.\"\n2. **Task list expanded**: plugs → bottle → petri dish rolling → wiping → casting pieces (Company ABC). Ziplock postponed.\n3. **Plugs experiment 1**: cluttered in a box (industry setting), instrument the sheet (not the plug), 6 sizes with train/test split.\n4. **Petri dish ≠ wiping** — rolling motion over objects is one task, wiping in S-motion is a separate task.\n5. **Bottle**: phototransistor instead of LDR, possibly multiple, 3D printed bottle variations.\n\nLet me now formalize everything task by task. I'll start with **Experiment 1 (plugs)** since that's the priority, then move through the rest.\n\n---\n\n## Formalized Task Descriptions\n\n### Experiment 1: Rubber Plug Insertion (Pilot)\n\n**Setup:** Multiple rubber plugs of varying sizes placed in a cluttered box on a metal sheet with holes. Robot must pick a plug and insert it into the correct hole.\n\n**Instrumentation:** Button-like contacts embedded in the metal sheet around each hole. When a plug is fully seated, the contact closes → binary signal.\n\n**Modality (hypothesis):** Audio — click/snap sound when plug seats is distinctive in spectrogram.\n\n**Success criterion:** Circuit closed (instrumentation = success signal).\n\n**Generalization axis:** Plug size.\n- 6 plug sizes total (size 1 through 6)\n- **Train on:** sizes 1, 3, 5\n- **Test on:** sizes 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\n- In-distribution (ID) = sizes 1, 3, 5\n- Out-of-distribution (OOD) = sizes 2, 4, 6\n\n**Evaluation split:** 10 rollouts ID + 10 rollouts OOD = 20 total per config.\n\n**Data collection:** Teleoperated with visual force feedback. Successful episodes only. Iterative collection (batches of 20) until from-scratch hits 90% or plateaus, cap 100.\n\n**Report for next meeting:**\n- [ ] Confirm: is instrumenting the sheet (not the plug) mechanically feasible? What's the exact construction?\n- [ ] Confirm: 6 sizes, train on 1/3/5, test on all 6\n- [ ] Confirm: cluttered box setup — how many plugs per trial? Different colors? Same color?\n- [ ] Suitability metric: pretrain audio vs image on held-out validation, pick winner\n\n---\n\n### Experiment 2: Bottle Opening\n\n**Setup:** Bottle with a flicking/switch turning cap mechanism. Robot must unscrew/open the cap.\n\n**Instrumentation:** Phototransistor(s) inside the cap. Continuous signal measuring how open the cap is. Multiple phototransistors for richer signal.\n\n**Modality (hypothesis):** Image — cap rotation is visible.\n\n**Success criterion:** Phototransistor reading above threshold = fully open.\n\n**Generalization axis:** Bottle type/geometry.\n- Multiple 3D-printed bottle variants\n- **Train on:** subset of bottle types\n- **Test on:** all types including unseen\n\n**Report for next meeting:**\n- [ ] How many bottle variants? How different are they (color, size, cap shape)?\n- [ ] Train/test split of bottle types?\n- [ ] Spring-based opening mechanism — who designs the 3D prints? (You mentioned uncertainty)\n- [ ] Phototransistor placement and calibration details\n- [ ] Success threshold for phototransistor reading\n\n---\n\n### Experiment 3: Petri Dish Rolling\n\n**Setup:** Petri dish with agar. Robot must press it in a rolling motion over objects on a surface. Objects can be flat, round, or arbitrary shapes. Policy receives target coordinates as input.\n\n**Full task sequence:** Go to object → open petri dish → rolling motion over target area → close petri dish.\n\n**Instrumentation:** Force-based (load cell). Measures contact force during rolling.\n\n**Modality (hypothesis):** Audio — friction/contact sounds during rolling.\n\n**Generalization axis:** Surface type.\n- 6 different surfaces\n- **Train on:** subset of surfaces\n- **Test on:** all surfaces including unseen\n\n**Report for next meeting:**\n- [ ] Train/test split of the 6 surfaces?\n- [ ] What are the 6 surfaces? (materials, textures)\n- [ ] Force range for \"correct\" rolling pressure?\n- [ ] How are target coordinates provided? (absolute image coordinates? relative to gripper?)\n- [ ] Success criterion: force in range during contact for sufficient duration?\n- [ ] Internal FT sensor dismissed by promotor as imprecise — confirmed?\n\n---\n\n### Experiment 4: Wiping (S-motion)\n\n**Setup:** After petri dish sampling, the area needs cleaning. Robot takes a tissue/cloth from a box and wipes in an S-shaped motion.\n\n**Instrumentation:** Vision-based. Camera overhead monitors a grid of points (5cm spacing). Reports current coverage index. Policy learns to visit next unvisited point — sequential like whack-a-mole.\n\n**Modality (hypothesis):** Image — coverage is directly visible.\n\n**Success criterion:** All grid points covered within tolerance.\n\n**Report for next meeting:**\n- [ ] Grid size (how many points? e.g., 5×5 = 25 points?)\n- [ ] How is \"covered\" determined? (gripper within X cm of point?)\n- [ ] Is this a standalone task or always paired with petri dish?\n- [ ] Generalization axis? (different grid sizes? different areas?)\n- [ ] Force importance — load cell also here, or just vision?\n\n---\n\n### Experiment 5: Company ABC Casting Pieces (long-term)\n\n**Setup:** Casting pieces that must fit perfectly into a mold/receptacle. Sometimes they don't seat fully on first attempt.\n\n**Instrumentation:** Distance sensor / ultrasonic sensor / camera-based. Measures gap between piece and receptacle.\n\n**Success criterion:** Piece fully seated (gap below threshold).\n\n**Report for next meeting:**\n- [ ] What kind of casting pieces? Size? Material?\n- [ ] What's the \"perfect fit\" tolerance?\n- [ ] Is the knocking/tapping action part of the learned policy or a fixed heuristic?\n- [ ] Generalization axis? (different piece geometries?)\n\n---\n\n## Concerns to Flag\n\n**1. Data efficiency + generalization conflation.** Your instinct was right. The 10 ID + 10 OOD split is smart, but in the paper you need to report them separately:\n- **Figure A:** Success rate vs. % data (ID rollouts) → data efficiency story\n- **Figure B:** Success rate ID vs. OOD at each data level → generalization story\n- These are two different claims from the same rollouts. Make this explicit.\n\n**2. Number of experiments is growing fast.** 5 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels × 20 rollouts = **1,200 rollouts** (~60 hours). Plus iterative N-finding for each task. This is a significant commitment. Is there a priority order beyond \"plugs first\"?\n\n**3. The \"casting pieces\" task feels different from the others.** It's about applying corrective force (knocking/tapping) rather than learning a manipulation trajectory. Does it fit the same experimental framework?\n\n---\n\nFor the next meeting, do you want me to:\n1. **Write up each task as a formal one-pager** (setup, instrumentation, generalization axis, evaluation protocol)?\n2. **Design the paper figures and tables** (what the results will look like)?\n3. **Both?**\n\nWhich do you want to tackle first?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":66353,"output":2455,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":68808,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1783505210582,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b98244fd3c38dd72"}}
