{"type":"message","id":"4391cfe4","parentId":"e8d5b52c","timestamp":"2026-07-08T12:30:39.204Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_5fb1c9e9b25448db8d6e719d","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Instrumented Imitation Learning — Experimental Design Report\n\n**Prepared for:** Promotor meeting\n**Date:** 1 July 2026\n**Status:** Design largely locked; open decisions flagged in §8\n\n---\n\n## At a glance — decision map\n\n*One-page overview of the full design and the open questions. A standalone landscape PDF (`Promotor_Meeting_Decision_Map.pdf`) is on the Desktop for screen-sharing during the meeting.*\n\n![Decision map: pipeline, tasks, and open questions](promotor_meeting_diagram.svg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question & hypothesis\n\n**Research question:** Does *instrumentation* improve policy performance and data efficiency in imitation learning?\n\n**Instrumentation** = task-specific auxiliary sensors added to the robot's *environment* that provide **privileged information available during training only, not at inference**. Examples: a rubber plug wired to close an electrical circuit when seated; an LDR inside a bottle cap reporting how open it is.\n\n**Refined hypothesis:** Supervised pretraining of a perception encoder on these visible-but-labelled instrumentation signals acts as an inductive bias, teaching the encoder to focus on task-relevant features faster than unsupervised/joint learning. The result is **better data efficiency** during policy fine-tuning — the same success rate reached with fewer demonstrations.\n\n> The signal does **not** need to be hidden from the camera/microphone. It only needs a *label* that accelerates representation learning. The instrumentation must be perceivable in the image or audio; otherwise the encoder learns only spurious correlations.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core contribution — the three-variant comparison\n\nThe heart of the paper is a controlled comparison of three policies that differ **only in how one encoder is initialized**. Everything else (architecture, data, training steps) is identical.\n\n| Variant | Pretrained encoder init | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **From scratch** | Random (image + audio) | Lower baseline |\n| **Instrumentation-pretrained** | One encoder pretrained to predict the instrumentation signal | The proposed method |\n| **Generic-pretrained** | Same encoder pretrained on generic data (ImageNet / AudioSet) | Controls for \"pretraining in general\" |\n\n**Why the generic baseline matters:** it isolates the claim. If instrumentation-pretrained beats generic-pretrained, the gain comes **specifically from the task instrumentation**, not from pretraining per se. The generic variant always mirrors the instrumentation variant's encoder choice (same modality), differing only in the pretraining *source*. All encoders are **fine-tuned** during policy training (not frozen) — same steps, same everything, only the initialization differs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tasks & instrumentation\n\nOrdered simple → complex, single-arm before bimanual. **Start with rubber plugs** to validate the full pipeline (collect → pretrain → train → evaluate).\n\n| # | Task | Arms | Instrumentation | Signal | Hypothesized modality* | Success criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Rubber plugs** in metal sheet | 1 | Metal ring on plug closes a circuit on the sheet (like a button) | Binary | Audio (seating click) | Circuit closed |\n| 2 | **Bottle opening** (flick-switch cap) | 1 | LDR inside the cap | Continuous | Image (visible cap rotation) | LDR > threshold |\n| 3 | **Disk wiping** at consistent pressure | 1 | Load cell under surface (or FSR under disk) | Continuous | Audio (friction sound) | Mean contact force in acceptable range |\n| 4 | **Ziplock bag** open/close | 2 (bimanual) | **TBD** — see §8 | Continuous | Image (slider position) | Slider fully open |\n\n\\* The hypothesized modality is only a starting guess — the actual choice is made objectively by the suitability metric (§4).\n\n**Instrumentation doubles as the success signal** for every task — either immediately (plug circuit) or after processing (wiping force). No separate evaluation logic is needed; the sensor that supervises training also defines test-time success.\n\n**Ziplock is deferred:** it is the only bimanual task (deformable, cannot be done one-armed) and is not directly comparable to the other three. It will be added *later, if time/budget allows*, and requires extending the single-arm framework.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Modality selection — the suitability metric\n\nTo avoid the \"you cherry-picked the modality that worked\" critique, the choice of which encoder to pretrain (image vs. audio) is made by a **pre-registered, objective test measured before any policy evaluation**:\n\n1. Split demonstrations 80% train / 20% validation.\n2. Pretrain the **image** encoder to predict the instrumentation signal → record validation accuracy / R².\n3. Pretrain the **audio** encoder the same way → record validation accuracy / R².\n4. **Pick the higher-scoring modality** for that task.\n\nThis yields a clean per-task table (Image score vs. Audio score → chosen modality) that justifies each decision on evidence rather than intuition.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Encoder pretraining\n\n- **Objective follows the signal type:** classification (BCE) for binary/discrete instrumentation (plugs); regression (MSE) for continuous instrumentation (bottle, wiping).\n- **Normalization:** continuous signals normalized to [0, 1], preferably by **percentile within the demonstration dataset**. The model then learns *\"is this force high or low relative to what's typical for this task?\"* rather than absolute newtons — which should generalize better across surfaces/materials.\n- **Pretraining data = the actual demonstration data** (not separately collected).\n- **Hyperparameters:** LR 1e-4, early stopping on validation-loss plateau. Cheap (~10–30 min/task).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Data-efficiency protocol\n\n- **Data-reduction levels:** 100%, 75%, 50%, 25% of N.\n- **Reduction method:** first N episodes (uniform prefix), assuming episodes are i.i.d. — chosen to avoid multiplying the number of runs by a k-repeat factor. *(Optional zero-cost robustness: shuffle with a fixed seed, then take the prefix.)*\n- **Both pretraining and fine-tuning use the same reduced subset.** This is the honest data-efficiency test: at 25%, *everything* sees only 25%. If instrumentation still wins, it proves the signal is more informative per sample — not that it secretly saw more data.\n\n### Finding N (total episodes) — iterative protocol\n1. Collect a batch of 20 demonstrations.\n2. Train the from-scratch policy on all episodes collected so far.\n3. Evaluate with 20 rollouts.\n4. Repeat until success ≥ **90%**, or a **plateau** is detected (≤ 5% improvement over two consecutive iterations).\n5. **Cap N at 100 episodes** to bound worst-case effort.\n6. N is then fixed; run the full experiment at 100/75/50/25% of N.\n\n**Successful demos only** — failed episodes are discarded during collection (so ~120 collected may be needed to reach ~100 successful). N is task-dependent, which is honest and expected.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Training, data collection & evaluation\n\n**Training protocol**\n- **Fixed number of training steps for all variants** (equal compute budget) — chosen over \"train to a target loss.\" Same steps = fair, simple comparison. This matches standard practice for Diffusion Policy (see *Training duration* below).\n- Fine-tuning uses identical hyperparameters/duration across variants.\n- **Framework:** lerobot (ACT / Diffusion Policy). Note: **Diffusion Policy has no memory**, so the instrumentation signal must be **instantaneous** (current-state force/contact), never cumulative (e.g. wipe *coverage* is ruled out).\n\n**Training duration — resolves the \"target-loss\" question (literature-grounded)**\n\nThe senior's suggestion to *train to a target loss ≈ 0.001 and reuse that step count* does not transfer to Diffusion Policy. Literature check:\n\n- **DP's training loss is not a convergence/quality signal.** It is the denoising MSE on the predicted noise ε, *averaged over random diffusion timesteps* (`num_train_timesteps=100`, `prediction_type=epsilon`). Because that average includes high-noise steps where the noise is essentially unpredictable, the loss **plateaus at a setup-dependent value and never decays toward ~0.001**. A 0.001 target is closer to an **ACT-style L1 action-reconstruction loss** — a different quantity. DP's absolute loss also shifts with noise schedule, prediction type, and action normalization, so it is not comparable across runs.\n- **The field trains to a fixed budget, not a loss.** The original DP repo uses a fixed `num_epochs`: **600** (real-robot image, UNet), **3050** (sim image, e.g. PushT/robomimic hybrid), up to **5000–8000** (lowdim / harder sim). lerobot (our framework) defaults to **`steps = 100 000`** with periodic evaluation every 20k steps.\n- **Checkpoints are selected by rollout success, explicitly not by loss.** DP configs set `checkpoint.topk.monitor_key = test_mean_score` (`mode: max`), evaluated every 50 epochs. Chi et al. (RSS 2023) state that low validation loss does not reliably track task success.\n\n**Decision:** Fixed step budget for all variants — pick a count where the **from-scratch variant at 100% data clearly plateaus in rollout success** (checked via periodic rollout eval), then reuse that same budget across all variants and data levels. Grounded anchor to start from: **lerobot's 100k-step default** (or DP's 600 epochs for real-robot image tasks).\n\n> Sources: `real-stanford/diffusion_policy` config files (`num_epochs`, `checkpoint.topk.monitor_key = test_mean_score`); `huggingface/lerobot` `configs/train.py` (`steps: int = 100_000`); Chi et al., *Diffusion Policy: Visuomotor Policy Learning via Action Diffusion*, RSS 2023.\n\n**Data collection**\n- **Teleoperation for all tasks** (not scripted) — one consistent method avoids confounding task type with data-collection method, and gives ecological validity + trajectory diversity.\n- **Real-time visual force feedback during teleop** (on-screen bar/needle with a target band) so the operator applies *intentional* expert force. Without feedback, recorded force is incidental and noisy.\n\n**Evaluation**\n- **20 real-world rollouts per configuration.**\n- **Primary metric: success rate** (secondary metrics dropped as non-essential — the success-rate curve is the story).\n- **Rollout timeout:** 2× median demonstration duration → mark as failure.\n- **Statistics:** 95% binomial confidence intervals per success rate; Fisher's exact test for pairwise variant comparisons at each data level.\n\n**Experimental scale (3 single-arm tasks first)**\n- 3 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels = **36 policy trainings** and **36 configs × 20 = 720 rollouts** (~36 h hands-on).\n- Adding bimanual ziplock later = +240 rollouts + framework extension.\n\n**Hardware:** 2× UR5 arms with Robotiq + Schunk grippers. Codebase: `robot-imitation-glue` (currently single-arm + gripper; needs extension for the bimanual ziplock task).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Open questions to resolve in the meeting\n\nThese are the decisions I deliberately deferred to you, promotor:\n\n1. **Ziplock-bag instrumentation.** No sensor decided yet. Options considered: Hall-effect sensor + magnet on the slider (recommended: minimal intrusion, continuous), string potentiometer, or capacitive distance sensor. A linear potentiometer was ruled out (too rigid, changes bag dynamics). *Decision needed.*\n\n2. **Wiping-task validity & force disambiguation.**\n   - If two surfaces are **visually identical but need different forces**, can **audio** (sliding-friction sound) disambiguate the required force? Needs a suitability check across materials.\n   - **Force tolerance:** if *any* force across a wide range works equally well, the task is too easy and unsuitable for imitation learning. We must confirm a meaningful, narrow force tolerance exists.\n\n3. **Generalization claim.** Current scope = **within-task data efficiency only** (same objects/setup, less data). Should we also promise **cross-condition generalization** (new materials, new bottles)? That roughly doubles the experiments. Is there pressure/expectation for it?\n\n4. **Scripted vs. teleoperated demonstrations.** Chosen: teleop for all (consistency). But deformables (ziplock) are hard to script, and mixing would confound results — so it must be **all-or-none**. Confirm teleop-everywhere is acceptable.\n\n5. **Training-duration criterion (align with senior).** Senior suggested training to a **target loss ≈ 0.001** and reusing that step count. **Resolved via literature (see §7, *Training duration*):** target-loss is not a valid anchor for Diffusion Policy — the field trains to a **fixed step budget** and selects checkpoints by **rollout success, not loss**. Proposed decision: fixed budget (start from lerobot's 100k-step default), sized so the from-scratch variant plateaus in rollout success. *Point to align on with the senior.*\n\n---\n\n## 9. One-paragraph summary of the method\n\n> *For each manipulation task we equip the environment with a task-specific sensor providing privileged information during training only. Guided by an objective suitability metric, we pretrain either the image or audio encoder to predict this instrumentation signal, then fine-tune the full policy. We compare three initializations — random, instrumentation-pretrained, and generic-pretrained (ImageNet/AudioSet) — across four data-reduction levels (100/75/50/25%), evaluating each with 20 real-world rollouts. If the instrumentation-pretrained encoder consistently reaches higher success with fewer demonstrations than the generic-pretrained one, it shows that task-specific instrumentation — not pretraining in general — drives the data-efficiency gain.*\n\n---\n\n## 10. Next concrete steps\n\n1. Resolve the §8 items with the promotor.\n2. Build instrumentation hardware for plugs, bottle, and wiping.\n3. Implement the suitability-metric pipeline.\n4. Run the rubber-plug task end-to-end first to validate the pipeline before scaling to all tasks.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1783513839204}}
{"type":"message","id":"3430fd4f","parentId":"4391cfe4","timestamp":"2026-07-08T12:31:56.055Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of everything. Let me write up the formal one-pagers for each task based on the new meeting conclusions. Key updates:\n\n1. **Scripted demonstrations** instead of teleop (because exact forces needed)\n2. **10 ID + 10 OOD rollouts** for generalization\n3. **Task list**: plugs → bottle → petri dish rolling → wiping → casting pieces\n4. **Petri dish**: internal FT sensor → predict external load (sidenote), 6 surfaces\n5. **Bottle**: same shape, different stiffness/look/stickers\n6. **Wiping**: standalone task\n7. **Casting pieces**: no knocking/tapping\n\nLet me also incorporate the concerns as flagged items.\n\nI'll write each task as a formal one-pager with:\n- Task description\n- Setup\n- Instrumentation\n- Modality hypothesis\n- Success criterion\n- Generalization axis\n- Train/test split\n- Open questions / to-dos\n\n\n- Now I'm ready to generate the report.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have full context. Let me write the formal one-pagers for all 5 tasks, incorporating every decision from the meeting and the concerns we identified.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8dca8905d5074637a0617d89","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"# Instrumented Imitation Learning — Task Specifications\n\n**Prepared for:** Promotor meeting (follow-up)\n**Date:** 8 July 2026\n**Status:** Task descriptions formalized; open items per task flagged with 🔶\n\n---\n\n## Shared Experimental Protocol\n\nAll five tasks share the same core methodology. This section is referenced by each task one-pager.\n\n### Research hypothesis\n\nSupervised pretraining of a perception encoder on task-specific instrumentation signals (privileged, training-only) acts as an inductive bias, yielding better data efficiency during policy fine-tuning than random initialization or generic pretraining (ImageNet/AudioSet).\n\n### Three-variant comparison\n\n| Variant | Encoder initialization | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **From scratch** | Random weights | Lower baseline |\n| **Instrumentation-pretrained** | Encoder pretrained to predict instrumentation signal | Proposed method |\n| **Generic-pretrained** | Same encoder pretrained on ImageNet (image) or AudioSet (audio) | Controls for \"pretraining in general\" |\n\nAll encoders are **fine-tuned** during policy training. Same architecture, same hyperparameters, same training steps. Only the initialization differs.\n\n### Modality selection\n\nAn objective **suitability metric** picks which encoder (image or audio) to pretrain:\n1. Split demonstrations 80/20 train/validation\n2. Pretrain image encoder → record validation score (accuracy for classification, R² for regression)\n3. Pretrain audio encoder → record validation score\n4. Pick the higher-scoring modality\n\n### Data collection\n\n- **Scripted demonstrations** — chosen over teleoperation because exact force profiles are required (petri dish, wiping tasks). Scripting ensures force precision and repeatability.\n- **Successful episodes only** — filtered by instrumentation signal at episode end.\n- **Iterative data collection** to find N (total episodes):\n  1. Script and collect a batch of 20 episodes\n  2. Train from-scratch policy on all episodes collected so far\n  3. Evaluate with 20 rollouts\n  4. Repeat until: success ≥ 90%, OR plateau (≤ 5% improvement over 2 consecutive batches), OR cap of 100 episodes\n  5. N is then fixed per task\n\n### Data reduction levels\n\n100%, 75%, 50%, 25% of N. Uniform prefix after shuffle with fixed seed. Both pretraining and fine-tuning use the same reduced subset (honest data efficiency test).\n\n### Training\n\n- **Diffusion Policy** via lerobot (image + audio encoders)\n- **Fixed step budget** for all variants (equal compute budget). Starting point: lerobot default of 100K steps. Checkpoint selected by rollout success, not training loss.\n- Pretraining: LR 1e-4, early stopping (patience 500), BCE for binary, MSE for continuous. Continuous signals normalized by percentile within demonstration dataset.\n\n### Evaluation\n\n- **20 real-world rollouts per configuration**, split:\n  - **10 in-distribution (ID)** rollouts — conditions seen during training\n  - **10 out-of-distribution (OOD)** rollouts — conditions NOT seen during training\n- **Success rate (%)** = primary metric\n- Rollout timeout: 2× median demonstration duration\n- Statistics: 95% binomial CIs; Fisher's exact test for pairwise variant comparisons\n\n### Paper reporting structure\n\nEach task yields two complementary claims from the same rollouts:\n- **Figure A (data efficiency):** Success rate vs. % training data (ID rollouts only)\n- **Figure B (generalization):** Success rate ID vs. OOD at each data level\n\n> ⚠️ **Concern:** Data efficiency and generalization are two different claims. They must be reported separately in the paper to avoid conflation. The 10+10 rollout split allows both without doubling total rollouts.\n\n### Experimental scale\n\n| Tasks | Configurations | Rollouts | Est. hands-on time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 tasks × 3 variants × 4 data levels = 60 configs | 60 | 60 × 20 = 1,200 | ~60 hours |\n| + iterative N-finding (~5 iterations × 5 tasks × 20 rollouts) | — | 500 | ~25 hours |\n| **Total** | **60** | **~1,700** | **~85 hours** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Concern:** 1,200 rollouts + N-finding is a significant commitment (~3 weeks of full-time robot operation). A priority order beyond \"plugs first\" should be confirmed.\n\n---\n\n## Task 1: Rubber Plug Insertion (Pilot Experiment)\n\n### Setup\n\nMultiple rubber plugs of varying sizes placed in a **cluttered box** on a metal sheet with corresponding holes. The robot must pick a plug from the box and insert it into the correct hole in the metal sheet. This mirrors an **industry setting** where parts are stored in bins and must be placed into fixtures.\n\n- **Robot:** UR5 + Robotiq/Schunk gripper (single-arm)\n- **Objects:** 6 plug sizes (sizes 1 through 6), placed in a single cluttered box\n- **Target:** Metal sheet with holes corresponding to each plug size\n\n### Instrumentation\n\n**Button-like contacts embedded in the metal sheet** around each hole. When a plug is fully seated, the contact closes, producing a binary signal. The sheet itself is instrumented (not the plug).\n\n- **Signal type:** Binary (0 = not seated, 1 = seated)\n- **Pretraining objective:** Binary cross-entropy (classification)\n\n### Modality hypothesis\n\n**Audio** — the click/snap sound when a plug seats into the hole is distinctive in the spectrogram. Visual cues may be ambiguous (occlusion from gripper, similar appearance before/after seating).\n\n### Success criterion\n\nCircuit closed (instrumentation = success signal). Binary, automatic.\n\n### Generalization axis\n\n**Plug size.** Six sizes total.\n\n| Split | Sizes | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Train (ID)** | 1, 3, 5 | Seen during data collection and training |\n| **Test (OOD)** | 2, 4, 6 | Unseen during training |\n| **Test (all)** | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 | Full evaluation |\n\n### Open items\n\n🔶 Confirm with promotor: mechanical construction of instrumented metal sheet (button contacts around each hole). Need the sheet and work out wiring.\n🔶 Confirm: how many plugs per trial? All 6 sizes in the box simultaneously? Or one size per trial?\n🔶 Confirm: plug colors — different colors per size (visual discrimination aid) or same color (force/tactile discrimination only)?\n🔶 Confirm: box dimensions, plug randomization per trial (reshuffled or fixed positions)?\n\n---\n\n## Task 2: Bottle Opening\n\n### Setup\n\nBottle with a **flicking-switch turning mechanism** for the cap. The robot must grasp the bottle and unscrew/open the cap through rotation.\n\n- **Robot:** UR5 + gripper (single-arm)\n- **Objects:** Multiple 3D-printed bottle variants\n- **Bottle variants:** Same shape, but varying in:\n  - **Cap stiffness** (tight vs. loose threading resistance)\n  - **Visual appearance** (some with stickers, some without, different colors)\n- **Bottle is fixed** in a clamp or holder (single-arm operation)\n\n### Instrumentation\n\n**Phototransistor(s)** inside the cap. Provides a continuous signal measuring how open the cap is. Multiple phototransistors may be used for a richer signal (e.g., different angular positions).\n\n- **Signal type:** Continuous (normalized to [0, 1])\n- **Pretraining objective:** Mean squared error (regression)\n\n### Modality hypothesis\n\n**Image** — cap rotation is visually observable. The encoder learns to correlate visual features (cap position, thread visibility) with the phototransistor reading.\n\n### Success criterion\n\nPhototransistor reading above a predefined threshold = cap fully open. Threshold determined during calibration (e.g., 95th percentile of \"fully open\" phototransistor readings across all bottle variants).\n\n### Generalization axis\n\n**Bottle variant** (cap stiffness + visual appearance).\n\n| Split | Variants | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Train (ID)** | Subset of variants (e.g., 3 of 6) | Seen during training |\n| **Test (OOD)** | Remaining variants (e.g., 3 of 6) | Unseen during training |\n| **Test (all)** | All 6 variants | Full evaluation |\n\n### Open items\n\n🔶 Confirm: how many bottle variants total? Exact train/test split?\n🔶 **3D-print design:** spring-based cap mechanism — who designs? (Operator expressed uncertainty about mechanical design confidence.)\n🔶 Phototransistor placement details and calibration protocol\n🔶 Success threshold value (to be set after calibration across all variants)\n🔶 Confirm: is the bottle clamped or held by the non-dominant arm? (Single-arm setup assumed.)\n\n---\n\n## Task 3: Petri Dish Rolling\n\n### Setup\n\nA petri dish containing agar is used to sample surfaces by pressing it in a **rolling motion** over target objects. Objects can be flat, round, or arbitrary shapes. The policy receives **target coordinates** as input so it knows where to position the petri dish.\n\n**Full task sequence:**\n1. Navigate to target object/location (coordinates provided as input)\n2. Open petri dish lid\n3. Apply rolling motion over target area with consistent force\n4. Close petri dish lid\n\n- **Robot:** UR5 + gripper (single-arm)\n- **Objects:** Petri dish with agar, various target objects\n- **Surfaces:** 6 different materials (see generalization axis)\n\n### Instrumentation\n\n**Load cell** under the wiping/sampling surface. Measures contact force during the rolling motion. Provides continuous force signal.\n\n- **Signal type:** Continuous (normalized by percentile within demonstration dataset)\n- **Pretraining objective:** Mean squared error (regression)\n\n### Modality hypothesis\n\n**Audio** — friction and contact sounds during rolling vary with force and surface. The encoder learns to correlate acoustic features with the load cell reading.\n\n> **Sidenote:** The robot's **internal force-torque (FT) sensor** could also be used. Instead of discarding it as \"imprecise,\" one could pass the internal FT sensor readings through an encoder and **predict the external load cell reading from the internal FT signal**. This would test whether the internal sensor, when properly learned, can substitute for the external instrumentation. This is an optional extension, not part of the core experiment.\n\n### Success criterion\n\nContact force within an acceptable range [F_min, F_max] during the rolling motion for a sufficient duration. Range defined from demonstration statistics (e.g., mean ± 1 standard deviation of expert force).\n\n### Generalization axis\n\n**Surface material.** Six surfaces:\n\n| # | Surface | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Tabletop (Thomas) | Standard lab surface |\n| 2 | Wood board with holes | Existing lab equipment |\n| 3 | Metal table | Rigid, reflective |\n| 4 | Plastic plate | Smooth, low friction |\n| 5 | White shelf wood | Painted wood |\n| 6 | Glass | Smooth, transparent |\n\n| Split | Surfaces | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Train (ID)** | Subset (e.g., 1, 3, 5) | Seen during training |\n| **Test (OOD)** | Remaining (e.g., 2, 4, 6) | Unseen during training |\n| **Test (all)** | All 6 | Full evaluation |\n\n### Open items\n\n🔶 Confirm: exact train/test split of the 6 surfaces (1,3,5 vs 2,4,6 or different?)\n🔶 Force range [F_min, F_max] for success — needs empirical determination from demonstrations\n🔶 How are target coordinates provided? (Absolute image coordinates? Relative to gripper? In what frame?)\n🔶 Internal FT sensor as optional side experiment — confirm scope with promotor\n🔶 Petri dish handling: how is the lid opened/closed with one gripper?\n\n---\n\n## Task 4: Wiping (S-motion)\n\n### Setup\n\nAfter surface sampling, the area needs cleaning. The robot takes a **tissue or cleaning cloth from a box** and wipes the surface in an **S-shaped motion**. This is a **standalone task** — not paired with the petri dish experiment.\n\n- **Robot:** UR5 + gripper (single-arm)\n- **Objects:** Tissue/cloth in a dispenser box, surface to clean\n- **Motion:** S-shaped wiping trajectory\n\n### Instrumentation\n\n**Vision-based coverage tracking.** An overhead camera monitors a **grid of reference points** (spaced ~5 cm apart) on the wiping area. The camera analyzes the image feed and reports a **progress index** — which grid point the gripper is currently nearest to. The policy learns to visit the next unvisited point in sequence, similar to whack-a-mole with sequential targets.\n\n- **Signal type:** Discrete/sequential (grid index, e.g., 1–25 for a 5×5 grid)\n- **Pretraining objective:** Classification (which grid point is being covered) or regression (normalized progress 0–1)\n\n### Modality hypothesis\n\n**Image** — coverage is directly visible in the camera feed. The encoder learns to correlate visual features (cloth position, surface cleanliness) with the grid index.\n\n### Success criterion\n\nAll grid points covered within a tolerance (gripper passed within X cm of each point during the wiping trajectory).\n\n### Generalization axis\n\n**TBD** — potential axes:\n- Different grid sizes (e.g., train on 5×5, test on 4×4 or 6×6)\n- Different wiping areas (different surface shapes)\n- Different cloth types (tissue, paper towel, microfiber)\n\n### Open items\n\n🔶 Confirm: grid dimensions (number of points? 5×5 = 25?)\n🔶 Confirm: how is \"covered\" determined? (gripper within what distance of each point?)\n🔶 Confirm: generalization axis for this task\n🔶 Is force instrumentation also relevant here (load cell for pressure)? Or vision-only?\n🔶 Cloth extraction from box — scripted or learned?\n🔶 Confirm: standalone task, not paired with petri dish\n\n---\n\n## Task 5: Casting Piece Placement (Company ABC)\n\n### Setup\n\nCasting pieces (industrial components) must be placed into a **mold or receptacle** where they need to fit perfectly. The robot must position and seat each piece precisely. Some pieces may not seat fully on the first attempt, requiring fine adjustments.\n\n- **Robot:** UR5 + gripper (single-arm)\n- **Objects:** Casting pieces, mold/receptacle\n- **Constraint:** Pieces must seat flush — no visible gap\n\n### Instrumentation\n\n**Distance sensor** (ultrasonic, laser, or camera-based) measuring the gap between the casting piece and the receptacle surface. Provides a continuous signal indicating how well-seated the piece is.\n\n- **Signal type:** Continuous (gap distance, normalized)\n- **Pretraining objective:** Mean squared error (regression)\n\n### Modality hypothesis\n\n**Image** — the gap between piece and receptacle is visually observable (shadows, depth cues).\n\n### Success criterion\n\nGap below a predefined threshold (piece fully seated). Threshold determined by the manufacturing tolerance of the casting pieces.\n\n### Generalization axis\n\n**TBD** — potential axes:\n- Different piece geometries\n- Different piece materials\n- Different mold/receptacle configurations\n\n### Open items\n\n🔶 Confirm: what kind of casting pieces? (dimensions, material, weight)\n🔶 Confirm: manufacturing tolerance for \"perfect fit\" (success threshold)\n🔶 Confirm: generalization axis\n🔶 Confirm: is this a priority task or long-term?\n🔶 Sensor choice: ultrasonic vs. laser vs. camera-based — trade-offs in precision and cost\n🔶 🔶 **Concern:** This task feels different from the others — it's about precision placement and force adjustment rather than learning a manipulation trajectory. Does it fit the same experimental framework, or should it be a separate study?\n\n---\n\n## Cross-cutting Concerns\n\nThese apply to multiple tasks and should be addressed in the paper's discussion section.\n\n### C1: Data efficiency vs. generalization\n\nThe 10 ID + 10 OOD rollout split serves two claims from one experiment. In the paper:\n- **Data efficiency** is reported using ID rollouts only (success rate vs. % data)\n- **Generalization** is reported as the ID/OOD gap at each data level\n- These are distinct claims and must not be conflated in the narrative\n\n### C2: Scripted demonstrations\n\nAll tasks use scripted demonstrations (not teleoperation) to ensure precise force profiles. This is a departure from standard imitation learning practice, where teleoperated demonstrations are the norm. In the paper, justify this choice: \"Scripting was necessary because force-critical tasks (petri dish rolling, wiping) require sub-newton precision that human teleoperation cannot reliably provide.\"\n\n> **Reviewer risk:** A reviewer might argue that scripted demos produce less diverse data, making the task easier and reducing the value of IL. Mitigate by showing that the policy must still learn to handle perceptual variation (different plug sizes, bottle appearances, surfaces).\n\n### C3: Task scope creep\n\nFive tasks is ambitious. Recommended priority:\n1. **Plugs** (pilot, validates pipeline)\n2. **Bottle** (confirms approach on a second task)\n3. **Petri dish** (adds force-critical dimension)\n4. **Wiping** (adds vision-based instrumentation)\n5. **Casting pieces** (long-term, industry partnership)\n\nConsider whether 3 tasks (plugs, bottle, petri dish) are sufficient for a strong paper, with wiping and casting as future work.\n\n### C4: Internal FT sensor as instrumentation proxy\n\nFor the petri dish task, the internal FT sensor could predict the external load cell reading. If this works, it raises the question: is external instrumentation always necessary, or can internal sensors suffice with proper representation learning? This is an interesting discussion point but should be clearly marked as an extension, not a core result.\n\n---\n\n## Reminders from previous sessions\n\n🔶 Literature search: empirically grounded training duration for Diffusion Policy (resolved: 100K steps, lerobot default, checkpoint by rollout success)\n🔶 Literature search: target loss values — resolved as not applicable for DP (denoising MSE, not action reconstruction)\n🔶 Check: force tolerance for wiping/petri dish (if any force works → task too easy)\n🔶 Discuss: visually identical materials → can audio disambiguate force?\n🔶 Implement: instrumentation hardware for all tasks\n🔶 Implement: suitability metric pipeline (pretrain image vs audio, compare)\n🔶 Implement: scripted demonstration framework for all tasks\n","path":"/home/raman/Desktop/Task_Specifications.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":73165,"output":4556,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":77721,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1783513839206,"responseId":"chatcmpl-84abcfee72bec467"}}
