{"type":"message","id":"63d9ee85","parentId":"ae3f34f1","timestamp":"2026-07-08T13:34:31.121Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to critically analyze the entire experimental design from a reviewer's perspective. Let me think through this systematically, going through each component and looking for weaknesses.\n\nLet me think about what a sharp reviewer at CoRL/ICRA/ICLR would attack:\n\n1. **Core hypothesis level:**\n   - \"Supervised pretraining on instrumentation labels acts as an inductive bias\" — but the pretraining data IS the same demonstrations used for policy training. So the encoder sees the same data twice (once for pretraining, once for fine-tuning). A reviewer would ask: \"Why not just add an auxiliary loss during policy training?\" The two-stage approach is harder to justify than a single-stage auxiliary loss.\n   - The generic pretrained baseline is also pretrained on the same data... wait, no. \n2. **Generic pretrained variant:**\n   - Generic pretrained loads ImageNet/AudioSet weights (trained on massive external datasets), while the instrumentation pretrained variant only trains on the small demonstration set. So the generic variant actually benefits from seeing orders of magnitude more data during pretraining. This means the instrumentation variant could theoretically win by learning task-specific features, but the comparison isn't perfectly balanced on pretraining data volume.\n   - Actually, this works in favor of the hypothesis—if instrumentation pretrained beats generic pretrained despite seeing less data, that's stronger evidence that the task-specific labels matter more than just having more pretraining data.\n\n3. **Scripted demonstrations:**\n   - Using scripted demonstrations is a major vulnerability because the field standard is teleoperated ones, and reviewers will push back on whether scripted demos have enough diversity or if the task is trivial enough to be scripted in the first place.\n\n4. **Data efficiency measurement:**\n   - Training on the same data twice (pretraining then fine-tuning) vs. training once from scratch means the pretrained variants actually get more gradient updates overall, so if they perform better it's unclear whether that's from the pretraining signal or just more optimization steps.\n\n5. **Fixed step budget:**\n   - I'm also concerned that a fixed training step budget might not be fair to from-scratch if the pretrained variants converge faster and end up overfitting while from-scratch is still learning.\n\n6. **Generalization claim:**\n   - Testing on only 3 held-out conditions (plug sizes 2,4,6 or surfaces 2,4,6) gives very limited evidence, and improving on OOD conditions could just mean the policy is more robust overall rather than specifically generalizing better.\n\n7. **Evaluation:**\n   - With 20 rollouts split 10 ID + 10 OOD, the confidence intervals get wide at lower percentages, and the success criterion for some tasks relies on threshold calibration that could be questioned.\n\n8. **Task selection:**\n   - The tasks span different complexity levels and modality pairings, making it hard to draw consistent conclusions across them.\n\n9. **Modality selection:**\n   - Picking the \"best\" modality per task introduces cherry-picking concerns—if instrumentation only works when you select the winning modality, the claim becomes weaker. A stronger test would fix the modality across all variants or pretrain both.\n\n10. **Diffusion policy architecture:**\n    - The architecture itself might be a confounding factor I haven't fully considered.\n\nThe diffusion policy processes image and audio independently before fusion, so pretraining one encoder doesn't directly affect the other or the decoder. That's reasonable. But diffusion policies are notoriously sample-inefficient, requiring thousands of demonstrations for real-world tasks—our cap of 100 might not be enough. And if the task is too easy with scripted demonstrations, a simple controller could solve it, which would undermine the claim that imitation learning is even necessary.\n\n11. **Normalization:**\n    - Normalizing by percentile within the demonstration dataset is problematic for small datasets, especially at 25% where the percentile distribution becomes unreliable. It also doesn't generalize well to OOD test conditions.\n\n12. **N-finding:**\n    - The iterative process to find N is fragile. If from-scratch never reaches 90%, the plateau detection becomes subjective. And since scripted demos are high-quality, from-scratch might reach 90% very quickly with just 20-30 episodes, making N small and the 25% condition tiny—maybe just 5 episodes.\n\n13. **Bottle task:**\n    - The task design itself has issues I'm still working through.\n\nThe bottle opening setup uses two arms but only one learns—the non-dominant arm holding the bottle in different positions acts as a confounding variable. If the pretrained variant learns to exploit specific arm positions rather than generalizing to bottle features, that's a problem.\n\n14. **Statistical power:**\n    - With only 10 OOD rollouts per condition, I can only detect relatively large effect sizes. If the true difference between variants is small—say 10% success rate improvement—I won't have enough power to detect it.\n\n15. **Ablation gaps:**\n    - I'm missing critical ablations. There's no test of pretraining without fine-tuning (frozen encoder), no comparison of pretraining on random labels to verify the signal itself matters, and no ablation showing what happens if I swap modalities.\n\n16. **Reproducibility:**\n    - The hardware-dependent nature of this makes reproducibility difficult. Each task requires custom instrumentation that other labs can't easily replicate.\n\n17. **Claim scope:**\n    - The claim about \"instrumentation improves data efficiency\" might be overstated given these limitations.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThis is exactly the right question to ask before the next meeting. Let me go through this systematically, ordered by severity.\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Critical (will kill the paper if unaddressed)\n\n### 1. The \"same data pretraining\" problem is deeper than we acknowledged\n\nYou pretrain on the demonstration set, then fine-tune on the *same* set. The from-scratch variant sees the data once; the pretrained variant sees it **twice** (pretraining pass + fine-tuning pass). A reviewer will say:\n\n> *\"The instrumentation-pretrained variant isn't data-efficient — it gets 2× the gradient updates. Compare fairly: give from-scratch the same number of total optimizer steps.\"*\n\n**Fix:** Total optimizer steps should be equal across variants. If pretraining uses 10K steps and fine-tuning uses 100K steps, then from-scratch should train for 110K steps. This is the single strongest attack on the core design.\n\n### 2. Scripted demonstrations — the \"why IL at all?\" attack\n\nA reviewer will say:\n\n> *\"If every task can be scripted, why do you need imitation learning? Script a controller directly. The paper's motivation collapses.\"*\n\nYou partially address this with the generalization claim (script fails on OOD), but the reviewer will counter:\n\n> *\"A well-designed scripted controller with closed-loop force/visual feedback also generalizes. Show that a scripted baseline performs worse than your best IL policy on OOD.\"*\n\n**Fix:** Add a **scripted baseline** as a fourth comparison. Run the exact same scripted trajectory at test time (without any learning) and report its success rate on OOD conditions. This shows where IL adds value over scripting. Even a simple open-loop scripted baseline strengthens the paper's motivation.\n\n### 3. The from-scratch baseline may trivially solve scripted tasks\n\nScripted demonstrations are clean, consistent, and low-noise. Behavioral cloning on clean data can converge very fast. A reviewer will ask:\n\n> *\"If from-scratch reaches 95% success at 100% data, the ceiling effect makes it impossible to show any variant is better. What happens if all variants tie at ~100%?\"*\n\n**Fix:** Add a **noise / perturbation protocol** to evaluation. E.g., randomize object positions beyond training distribution, add lighting changes, or use slightly different objects. This prevents ceiling effects and creates room to separate variants.\n\n---\n\n## 🟠 Strong (will weaken the paper significantly)\n\n### 4. Modality cherry-picking via suitability metric\n\nYou pick the \"best\" modality per task using a held-out validation split. A reviewer will say:\n\n> *\"You're selecting the modality that works. What if you had picked the other one? The claim should hold regardless of modality choice, or you need to show the suitability metric is robust.\"*\n\n**Fix:** Report *both* modalities' pretraining scores in the paper (not just the winner). Better yet: run the full experiment with **both** modalities for at least one task and show they converge to the same conclusion. This proves the suitability metric isn't gaming the result.\n\n### 5. Only 10 OOD rollouts — underpowered for generalization claim\n\nWith n=10, the 95% CI on a proportion is ±30 percentage points. If instrumentation-pretrained gets 60% OOD success and from-scratch gets 30%, the p-value from Fisher's exact test on 10 vs. 10 is ~0.18 — **not significant**. The generalization claim could be statistically unsupported.\n\n**Fix:** Either increase OOD rollouts to 20 (keeping total at 20 by dropping ID to 0 for generalization-only configs), or acknowledge this limitation and report effect sizes with CIs rather than claiming statistical significance for the generalization gap.\n\n### 6. Pretraining on 25% of data may be too little to learn anything\n\nAt 25%, you might have only 5-10 episodes (depending on N). Pretraining an image encoder on 5 images is meaningless. The instrumentation-pretrained variant at 25% is essentially random initialization, and any advantage vanishes.\n\n**Fix:** Consider a **minimum pretraining floor** (e.g., always pretrain on at least 10 episodes, even at 25% data level). Or argue that the result at 25% is expected to be weak and focus the paper's narrative on the 50% and 75% levels where the signal is meaningful.\n\n### 7. Percentile normalization breaks at small dataset sizes\n\nNormalizing continuous instrumentation by percentile within the dataset is unstable with small N. At 25% data (5-10 episodes), the percentile distribution is basically the identity function — no normalization happens. This means the 25% condition is not comparable to the 100% condition in terms of signal scaling.\n\n**Fix:** Use **global normalization** (min/max or z-score) fitted on the full N dataset, then applied to all reduction levels. This keeps the signal scale consistent across data levels.\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Moderate (reviewer will mention but not reject)\n\n### 8. N is task-dependent and unknown in advance\n\nN could be 30 for plugs (easy, clean scripted data) but 100 for petri dish (force-sensitive). Then 25% of plugs = 7 episodes, while 25% of petri = 25 episodes. The \"25% data\" condition means very different things across tasks.\n\n**Mitigation:** Report absolute episode counts alongside percentages. E.g., \"25% (N=8 episodes)\" not just \"25%.\"\n\n### 9. No ablation on the pretraining objective itself\n\nWhat if pretraining on *random labels* also helps (just from the extra gradient updates)? What if the benefit comes from the encoder seeing *any* supervised signal, not specifically the instrumentation?\n\n**Mitigation:** Add a **random-label pretraining** variant (pretrain encoder to predict random noise). If this doesn't help, it proves the instrumentation signal specifically matters. Cheap to add (same compute as main pretraining).\n\n### 10. Bottle task confounds arm position with generalization\n\nThe non-dominant arm holds the bottle in different positions. The generalization axis is bottle variant (stiffness, appearance). But if the policy learns to exploit specific arm positions rather than bottle features, the OOD generalization failure could be about arm positioning, not bottle properties.\n\n**Mitigation:** Ensure arm positions in OOD test are drawn from the *same distribution* as training. Only the bottle properties change.\n\n### 11. Wiping task's cloth extraction is learned but the rest is scripted\n\nThe cloth extraction is the most visually dynamic part of the task. If it's learned but the S-motion trajectory is scripted, the policy only learns one subtask. A reviewer will ask: \"is the S-motion scripted in joint space or task space? If joint-space scripted, the policy doesn't learn it at all.\"\n\n**Mitigation:** Clarify what the policy actually controls. If the full trajectory (extraction + wiping) is in the policy, great. If parts are scripted, state explicitly which parts are learned vs. scripted.\n\n### 12. Casting pieces task doesn't fit the framework\n\nAs you noted yourself. Different objective (force insertion vs. manipulation), different skill (compliance vs. trajectory following). Adding it to the same paper dilutes the narrative.\n\n**Mitigation:** Drop from the paper, keep as industry follow-up. Focus the paper on 3-4 coherent tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of top-3 things to fix before next meeting\n\n| Priority | Issue | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| 🔴 **1** | Pretrained sees data 2×, from-scratch sees 1× | Equalize total optimizer steps across variants |\n| 🔴 **2** | \"Why IL if everything is scripted?\" | Add scripted baseline as 4th comparison |\n| 🔴 **3** | Ceiling effect with clean scripted demos | Add evaluation perturbations (position, lighting, object variation) |\n\nThe rest can be addressed as paper-writing refinements. But the top-3 are deal-breakers if a reviewer picks them up.\n\nWant me to work through fixes for any of these, or shall we hold this for the promotor discussion?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":115961,"output":3009,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":118970,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1783517615020,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a0de8ea61788e7e5"}}
{"type":"message","id":"bfe0cd17","parentId":"63d9ee85","timestamp":"2026-07-08T14:26:23.237Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I will answer each of the points look at my answers critically, you can use searxng skill for webaccess if needed. on point1: the pretraining is not on the whole policy, only on the encoder used, compare the number of parameters of a resnet 18 to all parameters of a diffusion policy without the resnet18. Then this pretraining is just a small fraction of the weights that are updated isn't it? for point2: well then you need to instrument every environment, because one of the reasons we will be able to script so well is because of privileged infromation, the learned policies wil not have access to them. So your scripted baseline is not something I will do, we already have the expert demonstrations. for point 3, well iff all variants tie, our hypothesis is that the data efficiency curve that will have 25% 50 % 75% and 100% will exactly show that with less data instrumentation will help to learn faster than from scratch eventhough the task could be trivial. point 4 : I do not agree with the fact that the claim should be valid regardless of the modality choice, if a modality can not capture the instrumentation relevant features then instrumentation will not help in that case. That is my point. point 5 i would rather not do more rollouts, and also not remove the in distribution rollouts, so repotring the results and limitations is something I will do. point 6 : we will see, I doubt that N will be so small that we will have this issue, otherwise we will simply overfit I will see this in the loss curves and I will mention this in the results, no problem. point 7: maybe I will not normalize thne if this gives issues? point 8 : yes ofcourse I will also report absolute counts. point 9 makes no sense, how can you learn anything from noise? point 10: yes ofcourse I will do that. point 11: no I mean there is a misunderstanding, everything will be eventually be learned, the task of wiping involves getting the wipe out of the box, the demonstrations will be scripted but the final policy and task is learned. the getting wipe out of box will be scripted as well because it is part of the demonstration. point 12: casting will indeed be postponed for the future."}],"timestamp":1783520783235}}
{"type":"message","id":"7a3bd645","parentId":"bfe0cd17","timestamp":"2026-07-08T14:27:13.199Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user has responded to each critical point. Let me analyze their responses critically:\n\n1. **Point 1 (same data pretraining):** User argues that pretraining only updates the encoder (e.g., ResNet18 ~11M params) while the full DP is much larger. So the extra gradient updates are a small fraction.\n\n   Let me think about this critically:\n   - ResNet18: ~11.7M parameters\n   - A typical DP with image + audio encoders + UNet decoder: the UNet alone could be 50-200M params depending on config\n   - So yes, pretraining updates ~5-20% of total parameters\n   - But the reviewer's point isn't about parameter count — it's about the encoder seeing the data twice. Even if it's a small subset of params, those params get optimized on the data before fine-tuning begins\n   - The user's argument has merit though: the encoder params are a small fraction, and pretraining is cheap (converges quickly). The total compute is dominated by fine-tuning\n   - I think this is a defensible position, but the reviewer might still push back. A stronger defense would be: \"pretraining converges in ~10K steps on a small dataset, while fine-tuning runs for 100K steps. The encoder sees the data for an additional ~10% of its total optimization budget, which is negligible compared to the representation learning benefit.\"\n\n2. **Point 2 (scripted baseline):** User refuses because the scripted baseline would need instrumentation to work well, but the learned policy doesn't have instrumentation at test time. So the comparison is unfair.\n\n   This is actually a strong argument. The user is right: a scripted controller that uses instrumentation feedback is a different problem setup than a learned policy without instrumentation. The comparison would be apples-to-oranges.\n\n   The reviewer might push back by suggesting a simple open-loop scripted controller without instrumentation, but the user's reasoning holds up—instrumentation enables the high-quality demonstrations in the first place, so comparing against a non-instrumented baseline misses the point.\n\n3. **Point 3 (ceiling effect):** User argues the data efficiency curve itself shows the benefit— even if all variants reach ~100% at full data, the curve shows that instrumentation reaches that level with less data.\n\n   This is actually the core claim of the paper and it's solid. The data efficiency curve is exactly what matters here.\n\n4. **Point 4 (modality cherry-picking):** User's position is that if a modality can't capture the signal, instrumentation shouldn't help—and that's a valid finding, not a flaw. The claim is specifically about modalities capable of representing the instrumentation signal.\n\n5. **Point 5 (OOD rollouts):** User will acknowledge the limitation rather than collect more data. That's acceptable.\n\n6. **Point 6 (small N):** User will monitor loss curves for overfitting and report accordingly.\n\n7. **Point 7 (normalization):** User is flexible on the approach depending on what works.\n\n8. **Point 8 (absolute counts):** Agreed.\n\n9. **Point 9 (random labels):** User finds this unreasonable—you can't learn meaningful representations from noise.\n\n   Though I'm wondering if the real concern is whether any supervised pretraining helps, not just instrumentation-specific pretraining.\n\n10. **Point 10:** Agreed.\n\n11. **Point 11 (wiping):** User clarifies everything is learned—the demonstrations are scripted, but the policy learns the full task including cloth extraction. The demonstrations capture the complete trajectory from cloth extraction through wiping.\n\n    This reframes things: scripted demonstrations just means the data collection method is scripted, not that parts of the task are hardcoded into the policy.\n\n12. **Point 12 (casting):** Agreed, postpone.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering Point 9 more carefully. The reviewer's concern isn't whether you learn from noise—it's whether the benefit comes from the instrumentation signal specifically or just from having any auxiliary supervision. The real question is whether pretraining on random labels provides the same benefit as pretraining on actual instrumentation signals.\n\nI'm also circling back to Point 1. The user's argument about parameter counts is reasonable—encoder parameters are a small fraction of total compute—so this might not warrant major redesign. But I should flag that a strong reviewer could still push back, and suggest framing pretraining as initialization rather than additional training to strengthen the defense.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nLet me go through each response critically:\n\n---\n\n### Point 1: Encoder params ≪ total policy params\n\n**Your argument:** ResNet18 has ~11M params. A full DP with UNet decoder has 50-200M+. Pretraining updates a small fraction.\n\n**Verdict: Defensible, but needs framing.** You're right that encoder params are ~5-20% of total. But the reviewer's concern isn't total compute — it's that the encoder sees the data **before** fine-tuning, giving it a head start. Two episodes of optimization on the same distribution can matter for representations.\n\n**How to defend in the paper:** Frame pretraining as **weight initialization**, not \"extra training.\" The claim becomes: \"instrumentation-supervised initialization produces better starting weights than random or generic initialization.\" This is the standard transfer learning framing and is well-accepted. Also report pretraining steps vs. fine-tuning steps explicitly (e.g., \"encoder pretrained for 5K steps, then fine-tuned for 100K steps — pretraining represents <5% of the encoder's total optimization\").\n\n**But I'll flag one thing:** make sure pretraining doesn't overfit. With small datasets, an encoder can memorize the pretraining labels. If it overfits, the representations may be poor. This ties into your Point 6 response — monitor pretraining loss curves.\n\n---\n\n### Point 2: Refusing scripted baseline\n\n**Your argument:** Scripted controller would need instrumentation to work well; learned policy doesn't have instrumentation at test time. Unfair comparison.\n\n**Verdict: Correct. Drop this concern.** You're right — a scripted baseline with closed-loop instrumentation feedback is solving a different problem (supervised control with privileged info). An open-loop scripted baseline is trivially worse and uninteresting. The comparison is between learned policies, not learned vs. scripted.\n\nWhat you *should* add to the paper: explicitly state in the introduction that \"scripting is feasible for individual conditions but does not generalize across variations (plug sizes, surfaces, bottle types). Imitation learning is needed to learn a single policy that handles variation.\" This preempts the \"why IL?\" question without needing a scripted baseline experiment.\n\n---\n\n### Point 3: Ceiling effect\n\n**Your argument:** The data efficiency curve shows the difference even at the top.\n\n**Verdict: Solid, but only if the curve is separated.** If from-scratch at 25% data already reaches 80% success (because scripted demos are clean), and instrumentation-pretrained reaches 85%, the gap is tiny and statistically meaningless. The data efficiency claim only works if there's **visible separation** between curves at low data levels.\n\n**Risk:** Scripted demos are so clean that BC/DP converges fast even with little data. If N = 30 (plugs might be easy), then 25% = 7-8 episodes. DP on 8 clean episodes might already work well.\n\n**Mitigation:** Don't overthink this now. Run the plugs pilot, look at the curve. If it's flat (all variants similar), the task is too easy and you need to make it harder (more clutter, more plug sizes, occlusion). If the curve shows separation, you're fine.\n\n---\n\n### Point 4: Modality choice\n\n**Your argument:** If a modality can't capture the signal, instrumentation won't help. That's the point — not a flaw.\n\n**Verdict: Correct, and this is actually a contribution.** The suitability metric *is* part of the method. The full claim is: \"when instrumentation is perceivable in a modality (validated by suitability metric), pretraining on it improves data efficiency.\" This is honest and defensible.\n\n**What to add in the paper:** Report both modalities' scores in a table. Show cases where one modality clearly wins and cases where they're close. This transparency preempts the cherry-picking critique.\n\n---\n\n### Point 5: OOD rollouts\n\n**Your argument:** Won't do more rollouts, will report limitations.\n\n**Verdict: Acceptable.** Acknowledge in the paper: \"OOD evaluation uses n=10 rollouts per condition, limiting statistical power for the generalization claim. Results should be interpreted as directional evidence rather than statistically conclusive.\" This is honest and standard for real-robot experiments where rollouts are expensive.\n\n---\n\n### Point 6: Small N\n\n**Your argument:** Monitor loss curves, mention if it's an issue.\n\n**Verdict: Fine, but be proactive.** If pretraining on 5-10 episodes shows validation loss not decreasing (random performance), report that the 25% level is below the learning threshold and exclude it from the main figure (keep in supplementary). Better to present a clean 3-level curve (50/75/100%) than a 4-level curve where the lowest point is noise.\n\n---\n\n### Point 7: Normalization\n\n**Your argument:** Skip if it causes issues.\n\n**Verdict: Good instinct.** Use simple min-max normalization fitted on the full dataset, applied to all reduction levels. If that causes data leakage concerns (test sees train stats), use per-episode normalization (normalize within each episode). The simplest thing that works.\n\n---\n\n### Point 9: Random labels ablation\n\n**Your argument:** \"How can you learn anything from noise?\"\n\n**Critical counter:** You're right that the encoder won't *learn useful representations* from noise. But the reviewer's concern is different: **does any supervised pretraining signal help, or specifically the instrumentation signal?** If pretraining on shuffled labels (each image paired with a random force value) also improves data efficiency — even slightly — it suggests the benefit comes from \"having a pretraining objective\" rather than \"having the right pretraining objective.\"\n\n**My honest assessment:** This is a weak attack. Pretraining on shuffled labels almost certainly won't help (or will hurt via negative transfer). The reviewer is unlikely to demand this. **Skip it.** Your response is sufficient.\n\n---\n\n### Point 11: Wiping — everything is learned\n\n**Your clarification:** Demonstrations are scripted (data collection method), but the full task (cloth extraction + wiping trajectory) is in the demonstration data and learned by the policy. Nothing is hardcoded into the policy.\n\n**Verdict: This resolves the concern.** The key distinction is: **scripted demonstrations ≠ scripted policy behavior.** The policy learns everything from the data. Make this explicit in the paper: \"All demonstrations were collected via scripted robot trajectories to ensure force precision. The learned policy receives no scripted components — it learns the full task (cloth extraction, wiping trajectory, force modulation) end-to-end from these demonstrations.\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary of my assessment of your responses\n\n| Point | Your response | My verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Same data pretraining | Encoder ≪ total params | ✅ Defensible with proper framing (initialization, not extra training) |\n| 2. Scripted baseline | Instrumentation at test time makes it unfair | ✅ Correct, drop. Add motivation text instead. |\n| 3. Ceiling effect | Data efficiency curve shows separation | ⚠️ Depends on results. Pilot will tell. |\n| 4. Modality cherry-picking | Suitability metric is part of the method | ✅ Strong position. Report both scores. |\n| 5. OOD underpowered | Report limitations | ✅ Acceptable for real-robot work |\n| 6. Small N | Monitor, mention if issue | ✅ Fine |\n| 7. Normalization | Skip if problematic | ✅ Use min-max on full dataset |\n| 9. Random labels | Can't learn from noise | ✅ Skip, weak attack |\n| 11. Wiping scripting | Demo collection = scripted, policy = fully learned | ✅ Resolved. Clarify in paper. |\n\n**Remaining real risks (top 2):**\n1. **Ceiling effect** — scripted demos are so clean that from-scratch needs very little data. Only the pilot will reveal this.\n2. **Pretraining overfit** — on very small subsets (25%), the encoder may memorize labels instead of learning features. Monitor val loss.\n\nBoth are empirical — you'll know after running the plugs pilot. Nothing to fix in the design, just watch for during execution.\n\nWant me to update the Task Specifications document with any of these refinements, or are you ready to move on?"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":119472,"output":2772,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":122244,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1783520783242,"responseId":"chatcmpl-82b0bc06d292e722"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b1291a38","parentId":"7a3bd645","timestamp":"2026-07-08T14:36:01.973Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"point 1 is fair, add it to the report, yes. howeverremove the overfitting part because our modality suitability metric will measure validation performance so oferfitting is already accounted for in there. point 4 is also nice, add it. same with the point 7. Also for point 9 this is where the generic-pretrained policy will help, right?"}],"timestamp":1783521361972}}
{"type":"message","id":"822a08ad","parentId":"b1291a38","timestamp":"2026-07-08T14:36:09.785Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to:\n\n1. Add Point 1 framing to the report (pretraining as weight initialization, encoder params ≪ total, pretraining steps < 5% of encoder's total optimization)\n2. Remove the overfitting concern since the suitability metric already measures validation performance\n3. Add Point 4 (report both modalities' scores, suitability metric is part of the method)\n4. Add Point 7 (min-max normalization on full dataset)\n5. Address Point 9: the user is asking if the generic-pretrained variant already serves as the control for \"any pretraining helps\" question\n\nFor Point 9, the user makes a good observation. The generic-pretrained variant IS a form of pretraining - it's pretrained on ImageNet/AudioSet. So the comparison is:\n- From scratch: no pretraining\n- Generic pretrained: pretraining on generic data\n- Instrumentation pretrained: pretraining on task-specific data\n\nIf both pretrained variants beat from-scratch, it shows \"pretraining helps.\" If instrumentation beats generic, it shows \"task-specific pretraining helps more than generic pretraining.\" So the generic-pretrained variant already answers the question that the random-label ablation was trying to address.\n\n\nNow I need to revise the task specifications document to incorporate all these points and ensure the framing is consistent throughout.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nGood catch on Point 9. The generic-pretrained variant already answers that question: it's the \"pretraining on a different signal\" control. If instrumentation-pretrained beats generic-pretrained, the gain comes specifically from the *task-relevant* pretraining signal, not from \"having any pretraining.\" The three-variant design already covers this.\n\nLet me update the document:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f4a42e6ab3aa4f1a8f5a7ab7","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/raman/Desktop/Task_Specifications.md","limit":50}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B-FP8","usage":{"input":122332,"output":408,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":122740,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1783521361978,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bb3511b2fd3ec3da"}}
{"type":"message","id":"3019f511","parentId":"822a08ad","timestamp":"2026-07-08T14:36:09.801Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f4a42e6ab3aa4f1a8f5a7ab7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Instrumented Imitation Learning — Task Specifications\n\n**Prepared for:** Promotor meeting (follow-up)\n**Date:** 8 July 2026\n**Status:** Task descriptions formalized; open items per task flagged with 🔶\n\n---\n\n## Shared Experimental Protocol\n\nAll five tasks share the same core methodology. This section is referenced by each task one-pager.\n\n### Research hypothesis\n\nSupervised pretraining of a perception encoder on task-specific instrumentation signals (privileged, training-only) acts as an inductive bias, yielding better data efficiency during policy fine-tuning than random initialization or generic pretraining (ImageNet/AudioSet).\n\n### Three-variant comparison\n\n| Variant | Encoder initialization | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| **From scratch** | Random weights | Lower baseline |\n| **Instrumentation-pretrained** | Encoder pretrained to predict instrumentation signal | Proposed method |\n| **Generic-pretrained** | Same encoder pretrained on ImageNet (image) or AudioSet (audio) | Controls for \"pretraining in general\" |\n\nAll encoders are **fine-tuned** during policy training. Same architecture, same hyperparameters, same training steps. Only the initialization differs.\n\n### Modality selection\n\nAn objective **suitability metric** picks which encoder (image or audio) to pretrain:\n1. Split demonstrations 80/20 train/validation\n2. Pretrain image encoder → record validation score (accuracy for classification, R² for regression)\n3. Pretrain audio encoder → record validation score\n4. Pick the higher-scoring modality\n\n### Data collection\n\n- **Scripted demonstrations** — chosen because exact force profiles are required (petri dish, wiping tasks) and because scripting ensures repeatability.\n- **Diversity in scripted demos:** despite being scripted, diversity is maintained by varying conditions across episodes. For example, the non-dominant arm holds the bottle in different positions, the robot starts from different approach angles, or objects are placed at varied locations. The same underlying trajectory logic is parameterized over a distribution of conditions.\n- **Instrumentation aids scripting:** an additional advantage of instrumentation is that it provides ground-truth feedback that can be used to *guide* the scripted trajectory. For example, the load cell reading can close a feedback loop during scripted force application, or the circuit closure signal confirms when a plug is fully seated and the episode can end.\n- **Successful episodes only** — filtered by instrumentation signal at episode end.\n- **Iterative data collection** to find N (total episodes):\n  1. Script and collect a batch of 20 episodes\n  2. Train from-scratch policy on all episodes collected so far\n  3. Evaluate with 20 rollouts\n  4. Repeat until: success ≥ 90%, OR plateau (≤ 5% improvement over 2 consecutive batches), OR cap of 100 episodes\n  5. N is then fixed per task\n\n### Data reduction levels\n\n100%, 75%, 50%, 25% of N. Uniform prefix after shuffle with fixed seed. Both pretraining and fine-tuning use the same reduced subset (honest data efficiency test).\n\n[324 more lines in file. Use offset=51 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1783521369801}}
